WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.410 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.810 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: All
right, now my mic is on.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:15.350 align:middle line:90%
That has an effect on my voice.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.350 align:middle line:90%
I'm being amplified, right?

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.030 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.460 align:middle line:84%
So it'll be easy for
you to tell whether I've

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.010 align:middle line:90%
got my mic on or not.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.630 align:middle line:84%
So here's another
assimilation rule.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.270 align:middle line:84%
We said, in Arabic, there's
an assimilation rule-- wow,

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.980 align:middle line:90%
I'm really being amplified--

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.780 align:middle line:84%
in Arabic, there's
an assimilation rule

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.420 align:middle line:84%
that creates or
causes two consonants

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.780 align:middle line:90%
to be identical to each other.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:36.940 align:middle line:90%
This one is not that.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:42.400 align:middle line:84%
The consonant at the end of
that prefix is always nasal.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.010 align:middle line:84%
It just assimilates in place
to the following consonants.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.730 align:middle line:84%
You get "impossible"
and "incredible,"

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.762 align:middle line:84%
where you get a velar
nasal version of "n."

00:00:50.762 --> 00:00:52.720 align:middle line:84%
It's not "incredible"
[with alveolar nasal "n"]

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.420 align:middle line:84%
at least, unless you're
speaking very carefully.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:56.535 align:middle line:90%
That sound right?

00:00:56.535 --> 00:01:06.420 align:middle line:90%


00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.880 align:middle line:84%
Well, we're talking
about assimilation rules.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.818 align:middle line:84%
I want to show you another
example of vowel harmony.

00:01:11.818 --> 00:01:13.860 align:middle line:84%
We looked at one example
of harmony before, which

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.110 align:middle line:90%
was from Turkish.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:21.840 align:middle line:84%
And it was the observation that
the Turkish plural suffix is

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.470 align:middle line:84%
sometimes "-lar" and
sometimes "-ler,"

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.980 align:middle line:84%
and it depends on the
vowels that are in the noun

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.210 align:middle line:90%
that's being made plural.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:34.080 align:middle line:84%
So "lions" was "aslanlar"--
which I cannot spell all

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.480 align:middle line:90%
of a sudden.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.598 align:middle line:84%
But "roses," I
think, was "gyller."

00:01:42.598 --> 00:01:44.640 align:middle line:84%
It's either "roses" or
"flowers," I forget which.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.020 align:middle line:90%
Oh, we have a native speaker.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.960 align:middle line:90%
What does this mean, "gyller"?

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.220 align:middle line:90%
Is it "rose" or--

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.670 align:middle line:90%
"rose," OK, good.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.250 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, "aslanlar"
but "gyller"-- why?

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.710 align:middle line:84%
Well, because this,
we were going to say,

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.830 align:middle line:84%
is harmonizing with
the vowels that

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.180 align:middle line:90%
are in the noun in question.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.110 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, it's
deciding whether to be

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:11.310 align:middle line:84%
back or front depending on
whether the vowels in the noun

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.198 align:middle line:90%
are back or front.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:14.490 align:middle line:84%
So I want to show you another
example of vowel harmony,

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.350 align:middle line:84%
and we'll talk about
it a little bit more.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.030 align:middle line:84%
But that's what
vowel harmony was.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.430 align:middle line:84%
And as you can see, it's a
kind of assimilation rule.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.750 align:middle line:84%
So here's a vowel, and it's
deciding whether to be-- it's

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.650 align:middle line:84%
gaining its front or
back property for-- it's

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:33.210 align:middle line:84%
becoming the same as the
vowels that are in the noun.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.250 align:middle line:84%
So let's talk about
another vowel harmony case.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.500 align:middle line:90%
This is from Finnish.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.780 align:middle line:90%
So here are the Finnish vowels.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.400 align:middle line:90%
You can see it has--

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.500 align:middle line:84%
what does it have--
eight of them,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.200 align:middle line:84%
and they can all be long
or short, but long or short

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.690 align:middle line:84%
for this just means
how long you hold them.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.023 align:middle line:90%


00:02:51.023 --> 00:02:52.440 align:middle line:84%
Since we're talking
about Finnish,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:03:01.590 align:middle line:84%
I have to tell you my favorite
Finnish word, which is this,

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.380 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.750 align:middle line:84%
I was taught that
word by a linguist

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.070 align:middle line:90%
who specialized in Finnish--

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:11.313 align:middle line:84%
Erica Mitchell-- It's the
only Finnish word I know.

00:03:11.313 --> 00:03:13.230 align:middle line:84%
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]----
I figure it'll come

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.070 align:middle line:84%
in conversation at some point,
if I ever get back to Finland.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.308 align:middle line:84%
It means wedding
night intention.

00:03:19.308 --> 00:03:21.600 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not sure what kind of
conversation that will be,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.830 align:middle line:90%
but I have to work on that.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:25.900 align:middle line:84%
So there's the Finnish
vowel inventory.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.680 align:middle line:84%
One of the things you can
see about this inventory--

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.860 align:middle line:84%
it's got front vowels
and back vowels.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.510 align:middle line:84%
The vowels can be
high or mid or low.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:39.930 align:middle line:84%
And it is like English in
that its back vowels are

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.090 align:middle line:90%
rounded if they are not low.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:46.560 align:middle line:84%
So the back vowels
are "u," "o," and "a."

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.000 align:middle line:84%
And so it has a high back
rounded vowel-- for example,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:51.780 align:middle line:90%
"u."

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.190 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't have a high
back unrounded vowel, "ouh."

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:57.960 align:middle line:90%
So far so much like English.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.780 align:middle line:84%
Difference from
English is that it also

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.820 align:middle line:90%
has front rounded vowels.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.220 align:middle line:84%
So front high rounded vowel,
front mid rounded vowel,

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.590 align:middle line:90%
"eouh" and "ouh."

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.740 align:middle line:84%
So English doesn't have
those, but Finnish does.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.980 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, it also
has front high and mid

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.110 align:middle line:90%
unrounded vowels, "i" and "e"--

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.760 align:middle line:90%
so the pairs of vowels there.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.279 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to erase my
favorite Finnish word, just so

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:22.275 align:middle line:90%
as not to confuse later classes.

00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:25.780 align:middle line:90%


00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.700 align:middle line:84%
OK, so that's the
Finnish vowel inventory.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.570 align:middle line:84%
Now Finnish has vowel
harmony like Turkish.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:39.490 align:middle line:84%
It has a lot of suffixes
that harmonize with the noun

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.550 align:middle line:84%
and, in particular, Finnish is
kind of famous among linguists

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.520 align:middle line:84%
for having a lot of
suffixes on nouns

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.390 align:middle line:90%
that are thought of as cases.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:51.580 align:middle line:84%
So if you study languages
like Latin or Russian

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.905 align:middle line:84%
or I bet Ukrainian,
you need to learn

00:04:53.905 --> 00:04:55.780 align:middle line:84%
that nouns come in a
bunch of different forms

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:57.238 align:middle line:84%
depending on how
they're being used

00:04:57.238 --> 00:04:59.507 align:middle line:84%
in the sentence, the
nominative or accusative--

00:04:59.507 --> 00:05:01.090 align:middle line:84%
they get little
markers on them saying

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.580 align:middle line:84%
whether the subject or the
object or the indirect object

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:04.490 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.430 align:middle line:84%
This is what we call
the case of a noun.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.370 align:middle line:84%
Finnish-- languages
that have case,

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:13.120 align:middle line:84%
they often have two cases
or three or five or six.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.900 align:middle line:84%
Finnish, depending
on how you count,

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.750 align:middle line:90%
it has something like 12 or 13.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.460 align:middle line:90%
It's just nuts.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.580 align:middle line:84%
And that's partly because it
has a bunch of case suffixes

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:24.670 align:middle line:84%
that, in English,
would be prepositions.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.040 align:middle line:84%
So it has a case
suffix that means "on."

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.170 align:middle line:84%
This is the case
suffix that means "on."

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:30.685 align:middle line:84%
And it is either
pronounced "laa"

00:05:30.685 --> 00:05:32.977 align:middle line:84%
[WITH LOW FRONT VOWEL] or
"lah" [WITH LOW BACK VOWEL],,

00:05:32.977 --> 00:05:34.210 align:middle line:90%
depending on the noun.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.790 align:middle line:84%
So the word for "table"
is [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.032 align:middle line:84%
and "on the table" is
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:05:39.032 --> 00:05:40.990 align:middle line:84%
The word for "street" is
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:45.730 align:middle line:84%
and "on the street" is called
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.010 align:middle line:84%
I should be careful saying
things like the word

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:48.760 align:middle line:84%
for "street" is
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.700 align:middle line:84%
I think the word for "street" is
actually [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:51.828 align:middle line:90%
if you have it by itself.

00:05:51.828 --> 00:05:53.620 align:middle line:84%
But if you add the
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING],,

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.450 align:middle line:84%
it changes the "t" to a
"d," something like that.

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:56.560 align:middle line:84%
There are all kinds
of sneaky things

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.850 align:middle line:84%
that happen in Finnish, that
I forget, when you add them.

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:00.725 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, if you add
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:06:00.725 --> 00:06:03.010 align:middle line:84%
to street, you end up with
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.385 align:middle line:84%
If you add
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:06:04.385 --> 00:06:06.160 align:middle line:84%
to table, you get
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING],,

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:12.130 align:middle line:84%
and the change in the vowel
is conditioned by the vowel

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.920 align:middle line:90%
of the noun.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:16.870 align:middle line:84%
Now I've kind of
color-coded the vowels here.

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:20.350 align:middle line:84%
You can see there are some
front vowels that are red

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.610 align:middle line:84%
and some back vowels
that are blue.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:26.500 align:middle line:84%
And if you have a noun where
all the vowels are red,

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.290 align:middle line:84%
like "table," then the vowel
of the suffix is also red.

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.800 align:middle line:84%
You have a vowel where all
the-- sorry, a word where

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.440 align:middle line:84%
all the vowels are blue,
then the suffix is also blue.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:40.780 align:middle line:84%
So if all the vowels
in the noun are back,

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:43.570 align:middle line:84%
like in "the streets," then
the suffix gets a back vowel.

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:47.350 align:middle line:84%
If they're all front, then
the suffix gets a front vowel.

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:48.070 align:middle line:90%
So far so good?

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:50.630 align:middle line:90%


00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.700 align:middle line:90%
Now here's the thing.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.500 align:middle line:90%
There are also the green vowels.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.740 align:middle line:90%
Why do I have green vowels?

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.000 align:middle line:84%
The red vowels all have the
property that there is a blue

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.520 align:middle line:90%
vowel-- so a back vowel--

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.030 align:middle line:84%
that is identical to the
red vowel in every way

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.490 align:middle line:84%
except that it's back
instead of front.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.680 align:middle line:84%
So the red vowel
"eouh" for example,

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:18.630 align:middle line:84%
the front high rounded vowel,
has a corresponding blue vowel

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:22.620 align:middle line:84%
that is high and rounded
and different only

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:23.520 align:middle line:90%
in that it's back.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.500 align:middle line:90%
That's "u."

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.670 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, there is a
front mid rounded vowel,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.190 align:middle line:84%
"eouh," and there
is another vowel

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:33.690 align:middle line:84%
that is identical
to it in every way

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.565 align:middle line:84%
except that it's back
instead of being front.

00:07:35.565 --> 00:07:38.190 align:middle line:90%
That's oh.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:42.090 align:middle line:84%
But the green vowels don't
have any such correspondence.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:45.810 align:middle line:84%
So "i," the front
high unrounded vowel,

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.000 align:middle line:84%
there is no vowel which
is identical to that vowel

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.430 align:middle line:84%
in every way except
that it's back.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.470 align:middle line:90%
There isn't an "ooh."

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.460 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:57.090 align:middle line:84%
So the red vowels
and the blue vowels

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:59.850 align:middle line:84%
are identical in every way
except for front versus back.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:03.420 align:middle line:84%
The green vowels don't have
a corresponding back vowel

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:05.970 align:middle line:84%
that's identical to
them except being back.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:06.870 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.602 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:08.602 --> 00:08:10.560 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well,
it would be-- let's see,

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.930 align:middle line:90%
it would be "oueh."

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:14.490 align:middle line:84%
It's not a popular
vowel, but there

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:17.940 align:middle line:90%
are languages that have it.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.770 align:middle line:84%
The IPA symbol for it, I
think, is something like this.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:24.540 align:middle line:90%
So it exists.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.640 align:middle line:84%
We haven't talked about
a language that has it.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:29.150 align:middle line:90%


00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:32.331 align:middle line:84%
I'll erase it quickly
before it upsets anybody.

00:08:32.331 --> 00:08:33.789 align:middle line:84%
So it's physically
possible to make

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:35.747 align:middle line:84%
these vowels in their
languages that have them,

00:08:35.747 --> 00:08:37.100 align:middle line:90%
but Finnish doesn't have them.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:38.558 align:middle line:84%
And so the question
is, what do you

00:08:38.558 --> 00:08:42.760 align:middle line:84%
do when you add a suffix to
a noun that has these vowels?

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.790 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is that these
vessels are just invisible,

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:46.630 align:middle line:90%
basically.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:51.250 align:middle line:84%
So at the station is the
less interesting case--

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.200 align:middle line:84%
you have a couple
of front vowels

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.933 align:middle line:84%
that do have corresponding
back vowels, "y" and "ae,"

00:08:55.933 --> 00:08:57.850 align:middle line:84%
and then you have this
"i," which doesn't have

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:58.980 align:middle line:90%
a corresponding back vowel.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:00.730 align:middle line:84%
You have two red vowels
and a green vowel.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.240 align:middle line:84%
And you get a red
vowel in the suffix,

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.615 align:middle line:84%
so you get
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:09:03.615 --> 00:09:06.210 align:middle line:90%
for at the station.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:10.610 align:middle line:84%
But for "on the child,"
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING],, well,

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:14.195 align:middle line:84%
you have a blue vowel
that is a back low vowel,

00:09:14.195 --> 00:09:17.750 align:middle line:84%
"a," and then you have a
green vowel, which is front,

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.810 align:middle line:84%
but the green vowel doesn't
have a corresponding back vowel,

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.670 align:middle line:84%
and so it's like
it doesn't count.

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:25.080 align:middle line:84%
The green vowels can
go with anything.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.470 align:middle line:84%
So you have vowel harmony
for the vowels that

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:30.153 align:middle line:84%
have corresponding
front and back versions,

00:09:30.153 --> 00:09:32.570 align:middle line:84%
but for those that don't have
corresponding front and back

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:35.030 align:middle line:84%
versions, it's like
you get to ignore them.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:37.580 align:middle line:90%
They're not there.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:38.200 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:39.030 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense?

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.070 align:middle line:90%
So there are--

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:44.720 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's as though you
can say as long as you only

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.780 align:middle line:84%
look at blue and red vowels,
all the vowels in a word

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.700 align:middle line:90%
must be blue or red.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:51.722 align:middle line:84%
But you don't look
at green vowels.

00:09:51.722 --> 00:09:53.180 align:middle line:84%
You don't look at
vowels that don't

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:55.970 align:middle line:84%
have a corresponding back vowel
that is identical in every way

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:58.445 align:middle line:90%
except for being back.

00:09:58.445 --> 00:09:59.820 align:middle line:84%
So you get
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:02.940 align:middle line:90%
for "on the child."

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.570 align:middle line:84%
There are also words in which
all the vowels are green,

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.410 align:middle line:84%
like "at the
family," and the fact

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:14.070 align:middle line:84%
is that for suffixes, you get
front vowels corresponding

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:14.640 align:middle line:90%
to those.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:17.070 align:middle line:84%
And the green vowels
are front, which

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:18.400 align:middle line:90%
is itself kind of interesting.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.150 align:middle line:84%
So at the family is
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:22.380 align:middle line:90%
You get "lah" for that one.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:25.057 align:middle line:90%


00:10:25.057 --> 00:10:25.640 align:middle line:90%
Is this clear?

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:27.200 align:middle line:90%
That's the Finnish system.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.180 align:middle line:84%
If any of you ever want
to go learn Finnish,

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:30.290 align:middle line:90%
you're now all set up.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:31.415 align:middle line:90%
Does anybody speak Finnish?

00:10:31.415 --> 00:10:33.035 align:middle line:84%
Here I am pretending
to speak Finnish.

00:10:33.035 --> 00:10:35.880 align:middle line:90%


00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:40.260 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so just to summarize that
again, if a vowel is not round

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:44.595 align:middle line:90%
and not low, then it is front.

00:10:44.595 --> 00:10:49.030 align:middle line:90%


00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:52.720 align:middle line:84%
So one way to think about
what's going on in these suffix

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:54.580 align:middle line:84%
versions at the bottom
is to say there's

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:57.520 align:middle line:84%
a rule saying give all
the vowels in the word

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:01.870 align:middle line:84%
the same value for back, "o,"
except don't create vowels

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:03.250 align:middle line:90%
that don't exist in Finnish.

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:10.300 align:middle line:84%
So when you're doing "on the
child," it would be nice if--

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:13.150 align:middle line:84%
the fact is that you've
got a back vowel and then

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.880 align:middle line:90%
a vowel and then a back vowel.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:19.530 align:middle line:84%
But there's nothing you can
do about the front vowel, eh.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:22.840 align:middle line:84%
There isn't a corresponding
back vowel in Finnish.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:25.220 align:middle line:90%
So it's like the rules are--

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:31.500 align:middle line:84%
first of all, remember
Optimality Theory.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:34.350 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, the
generalization goes,

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:36.360 align:middle line:84%
give all the vowels in
the word the same value

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.240 align:middle line:84%
for back, but don't
create non-Finnish vowels.

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:43.620 align:middle line:84%
Don't create back vowels that
are not round and not low.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:45.030 align:middle line:90%
Don't create "ooh" or "eouh."

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.557 align:middle line:84%
There's that symbol you
were wondering about.

00:11:47.557 --> 00:11:49.140 align:middle line:84%
If we want to talk
about this in terms

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:50.515 align:middle line:84%
of Optimality
Theory, what we say

00:11:50.515 --> 00:11:54.810 align:middle line:84%
is the most important
thing is don't create back

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:56.250 align:middle line:90%
non-low unrounded vowels.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:57.900 align:middle line:90%
Don't create "ooh" or "eouh."

00:11:57.900 --> 00:11:59.400 align:middle line:84%
Finnish doesn't
have those vowels.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:00.392 align:middle line:90%
Don't do that.

00:12:00.392 --> 00:12:02.850 align:middle line:84%
But as long as you-- so that's
the most important priority.

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:04.433 align:middle line:84%
That's the highest
ranking constraint.

00:12:04.433 --> 00:12:06.460 align:middle line:84%
But as long as
you don't do that,

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:09.270 align:middle line:84%
make sure all the vowels
have the same value for back.

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.387 align:middle line:84%
They're all either
back or front.

00:12:11.387 --> 00:12:13.720 align:middle line:84%
That's one way to talk about
what's going on in Finnish.

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:18.070 align:middle line:90%


00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:21.430 align:middle line:84%
Now there's a popular approach
to phenomena like vowel harmony

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:24.070 align:middle line:84%
that I just wanted to
introduce you to, partly

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:26.560 align:middle line:84%
because a version of it will
float up again when we talk

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:31.180 align:middle line:84%
about syntax, I think,
which involves saying

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.620 align:middle line:84%
it's as though-- and
actually semantics as well--

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:40.840 align:middle line:84%
it's as though we want to talk
as though features of sounds

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:42.853 align:middle line:84%
can sometimes show up
in multiple places.

00:12:42.853 --> 00:12:45.020 align:middle line:84%
So here's the Finnish word
for "on the table" again,

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:46.900 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:52.410 align:middle line:84%
And if you were listing all
of the properties of all

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:55.063 align:middle line:84%
of those vowels-- that's
what I've done here,

00:12:55.063 --> 00:12:56.730 align:middle line:84%
and I just haven't
listed the consonants

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:58.800 align:middle line:84%
just because there
wasn't room and I'm not

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:00.030 align:middle line:90%
good at graphic design.

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:06.930 align:middle line:84%
But first of all, "ouh," is
round and mid and not back.

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:08.400 align:middle line:90%
That is front.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:12.600 align:middle line:84%
And the second vowel is
round and high and not back.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:13.890 align:middle line:90%
That is front.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:18.660 align:middle line:84%
And "ae" is not round and
is low, and is not back.

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:19.840 align:middle line:90%
That is, it's front.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:21.840 align:middle line:84%
This is just a little
list of all the properties

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:24.570 align:middle line:90%
of each of those vowels.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:27.960 align:middle line:84%
And so you could specify
all of these properties

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.210 align:middle line:90%
of these vowels this way.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:32.700 align:middle line:84%
But there's a popular
thought about what's

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:37.530 align:middle line:84%
going on here, which is to say
it's as though Finnish wants

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:42.570 align:middle line:84%
the value of back to be kind
of shared among all the vowels.

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:44.640 align:middle line:84%
That is, you're just
going to state it once.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:48.510 align:middle line:84%
This is a word in which
all the vowels are front

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:51.250 align:middle line:90%
or all the vowels are back.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:55.650 align:middle line:84%
So you say somewhere, Finnish
doesn't allow you to state over

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:57.942 align:middle line:84%
and over again this vowel is
front and this vowel front

00:13:57.942 --> 00:13:59.942 align:middle line:84%
and this vowel is front
and this vowel is front,

00:13:59.942 --> 00:14:02.040 align:middle line:84%
you just have to say
once, for the whole word,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.510 align:middle line:90%
these vowels are front.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:05.160 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
this thing about, oh,

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:06.868 align:middle line:84%
and I don't like
certain kinds of vowels,

00:14:06.868 --> 00:14:08.550 align:middle line:84%
so those vowels get
to be specified.

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:13.260 align:middle line:84%
But words just specify
whether they're

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:14.640 align:middle line:90%
front words or back words.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.650 align:middle line:84%
You don't specify it
over and over again

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:20.050 align:middle line:90%
from one vowel to the next.

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:21.993 align:middle line:84%
This is one popular way
of talking about how

00:14:21.993 --> 00:14:23.160 align:middle line:90%
we want to do vowel harmony.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.350 align:middle line:90%


00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:28.890 align:middle line:84%
It's as though there's only
one back feature in the word,

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:30.840 align:middle line:84%
and all the vowels
that can share it do.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:34.850 align:middle line:90%


00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:38.570 align:middle line:84%
The name for this-- it's called
autosegmental representation.

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:41.000 align:middle line:84%
This is the kind
of moment where I

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:45.770 align:middle line:84%
think I'm swimming against the
stream of your normal instincts

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:46.310 align:middle line:90%
for classes.

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:50.180 align:middle line:84%
This is a class in which
there aren't quizzes or exams,

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.940 align:middle line:84%
so putting something
like that up on the slide

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:56.210 align:middle line:84%
is triggering all your instincts
to write that down and memorize

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.970 align:middle line:84%
it because you figure
it'll come up on the test,

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:00.810 align:middle line:90%
but there is no test.

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:03.148 align:middle line:84%
So memorize that if
you would like to,

00:15:03.148 --> 00:15:04.940 align:middle line:84%
if you want to impress
linguists at parties

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:06.830 align:middle line:84%
or if you want to
make your TA happy,

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.030 align:middle line:90%
but there won't be a test.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:11.390 align:middle line:84%
You will never be
counted off on the paper

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:13.970 align:middle line:84%
for failing to use the phrase
autosegmental representation.

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:16.520 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, it is a way
to impress people at parties.

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:20.590 align:middle line:90%


00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:22.360 align:middle line:90%
So that's one form of harmony.

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:24.280 align:middle line:90%
It's called vowel harmony.

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:26.290 align:middle line:84%
We've seen it now in
Turkish and in Finnish.

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.480 align:middle line:90%
Those are not related languages.

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:30.700 align:middle line:84%
Vowel harmony is
crosslinguistically not--

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:34.840 align:middle line:84%
it's not hugely common,
but it's not rare either.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:36.730 align:middle line:84%
It's popular in
the Turkic family.

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:39.700 align:middle line:84%
There's a very large
family of languages

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:41.620 align:middle line:84%
that are related to
each other and they all

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:43.220 align:middle line:90%
have various kinds of harmony.

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:45.470 align:middle line:84%
There's lots of interesting
work on exactly what kinds

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:47.360 align:middle line:84%
of harmony you get
in which languages.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:49.510 align:middle line:84%
I want to show you
another kind of harmony

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:50.980 align:middle line:90%
which is a lot rarer.

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:53.260 align:middle line:84%
This is from a language,
the Navajo language,

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:58.020 align:middle line:84%
which is an Indigenous language
of the American Southwest.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.690 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch
of verbs in Navajo

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:04.740 align:middle line:84%
in what we could think
of as the past tense.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:08.670 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that
all of these verbs--

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:11.960 align:middle line:84%
so what I've done is
boldfaced a part of the verb

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:14.460 align:middle line:84%
that you can think of as the
part of the verb that tells you

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:15.720 align:middle line:90%
who the subject is.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:20.070 align:middle line:84%
So there's a morpheme "sé"
that's showing up in the first

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:24.660 align:middle line:84%
three verbs, which tells
you that the subject is "I."

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:29.550 align:middle line:84%
And there's a morpheme "síní"
in the next trio of verbs that

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.140 align:middle line:84%
tells you that the
subject is "you."

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:35.040 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a morpheme
"soo" in the last three verbs.

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:40.638 align:middle line:84%
It tells you that the subject
is "you two"-- that's you dual.

00:16:40.638 --> 00:16:42.180 align:middle line:84%
To say that this is
a single morpheme

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:43.650 align:middle line:90%
is a little bit misleading.

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:45.390 align:middle line:84%
The "s" at the beginning
of this morpheme

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.510 align:middle line:84%
is probably partly a marker
of what I'm translating here

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:50.520 align:middle line:90%
as past tense.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:54.660 align:middle line:84%
Navajo verbs are
extraordinarily complicated.

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:58.050 align:middle line:84%
So these particular
verbs are in a class

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:01.290 align:middle line:84%
that takes this "s" version
of the past tense, what

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:02.400 align:middle line:90%
are called s perfectives.

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:03.720 align:middle line:90%
There are also y perfectives.

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:04.829 align:middle line:84%
There are other
kinds of perfectives.

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:06.450 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, this
class of verbs takes

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:09.410 align:middle line:84%
these past tense morphemes, and
this is what they look like.

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:11.160 align:middle line:84%
Are there any questions
about any of this?

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:11.996 align:middle line:90%
Navajo has tone.

00:17:11.996 --> 00:17:13.829 align:middle line:84%
That's what those markers
are on the vowels.

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:17.099 align:middle line:90%
So the markers mark high tone.

00:17:17.099 --> 00:17:19.470 align:middle line:84%
There's another
marker that you're

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:24.630 align:middle line:84%
seeing underneath the vowel
in the verb for "stand."

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:26.670 align:middle line:90%
That's a marker of nasalization.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:30.300 align:middle line:84%
So "I stood" is something
like [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:34.620 align:middle line:84%
So you have to nasalize
the "eh," say that.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:35.490 align:middle line:90%
Here's some Navajo.

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:38.300 align:middle line:90%


00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:40.520 align:middle line:90%
Here's some more Navajo.

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:43.730 align:middle line:84%
"I boiled it" is
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:46.280 align:middle line:84%
"You boiled it" is
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING],,

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:51.080 align:middle line:84%
and "You two boiled it"
is [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:54.830 align:middle line:84%
So you can see, in the
three verbs that I had

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:57.500 align:middle line:84%
on the previous slide and
that are still up here,

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:01.270 align:middle line:84%
there are these three morphemes,
"sé," "síní," and "soo."

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:04.200 align:middle line:84%
But for "boil," the morphemes
are [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:07.200 align:middle line:90%
and [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:12.130 align:middle line:90%
This is called sibilant harmony.

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:16.180 align:middle line:84%
What's going on here-- and
here's another example of it--

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:20.740 align:middle line:84%
you get "sé," "síní," and
"soo" for "played" and "stood"

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:23.560 align:middle line:84%
and "crawled around," but
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:27.340 align:middle line:84%
and [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]
for "boiled" and "worked."

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:30.550 align:middle line:84%
And the relevant difference
between "boil" and "work"

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:32.890 align:middle line:84%
on the one hand and
"play," "crawl around,"

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:35.770 align:middle line:84%
and "stand" on the other
is that "boil" and "work"

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:40.420 align:middle line:84%
have, in the verb, a sibilant,
which is postalveolar.

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:42.790 align:middle line:84%
So "boil"-- the
stem you can think

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.370 align:middle line:84%
of as [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING],,
and "work"--

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:47.710 align:middle line:84%
its' [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]
is the verb stem,

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:49.600 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:54.160 align:middle line:84%
So "boil" has a "zh" in it
and "work" has a "sh" in it,

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:57.115 align:middle line:84%
whereas "play" and "crawl
around" and "stand" either

00:18:57.115 --> 00:18:59.740 align:middle line:84%
don't have sibilance in them at
all or, in the case of "stand,"

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:02.170 align:middle line:84%
it has a sibilant, but it's
not a postalveolar sibilant,

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:05.440 align:middle line:90%
It's an alveolar sibilant, "z."

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:07.475 align:middle line:84%
This is called
sibilant harmony, or I

00:19:07.475 --> 00:19:09.100 align:middle line:84%
think we've also
called these strident.

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:11.120 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, that's the
term you're familiar with.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:14.890 align:middle line:84%
So this phenomenon is
called sibilant harmony--

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:17.860 align:middle line:84%
so sibilant is another
name for strident, sorry.

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:19.640 align:middle line:84%
So let me just say
all that again,

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:23.260 align:middle line:84%
there are these postalveolar
stridents, "zh" and "sh"

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:26.260 align:middle line:84%
in "boil" and "work," and those
are triggering the postalveolar

00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:28.660 align:middle line:84%
versions of these morphemes,
the [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.030 align:middle line:84%
and [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]
instead of "sé," "síní,"

00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:33.690 align:middle line:90%
and "soo."

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:36.787 align:middle line:84%
I started this by saying vowel
harmony is pretty common.

00:19:36.787 --> 00:19:38.370 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
work on vowel harmony

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:40.495 align:middle line:84%
because you find it in lots
of unrelated languages.

00:19:40.495 --> 00:19:42.840 align:middle line:90%
Sibilant harmony is quite rare.

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:43.520 align:middle line:90%
Navajo has it.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:45.150 align:middle line:90%
A few other languages have it.

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:51.330 align:middle line:84%
But it's similar in the sense
that it looks as though,

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.940 align:middle line:90%
if you have a sibling--

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:56.460 align:middle line:84%
morphemes in these
prefixes whose

00:19:56.460 --> 00:20:02.580 align:middle line:84%
form is being conditioned
by corresponding sounds

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:03.420 align:middle line:90%
in the verb stem.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:05.910 align:middle line:84%
So for vowel harmony,
it's the vowels.

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:08.100 align:middle line:84%
For sibilant harmony,
it's these stridents.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:12.240 align:middle line:84%
So if there's a strident or
sibilant, an "s," "z," "sh,"

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:14.610 align:middle line:84%
or "zh"-- those are
the Navajo sibilants--

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:17.130 align:middle line:84%
in the verb stem, then any of
the sibilance and the subject

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:19.198 align:middle line:90%
prefix have to match it.

00:20:19.198 --> 00:20:20.490 align:middle line:90%
They're getting the same thing.

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:25.580 align:middle line:90%


00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:27.170 align:middle line:84%
Vowel harmony and
maybe this idea

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:29.767 align:middle line:84%
of sharing a feature
across sounds--

00:20:29.767 --> 00:20:31.100 align:middle line:90%
it's not just for vowel harmony.

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.830 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's for
assimilation generally.

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:34.580 align:middle line:84%
There might be some
other kinds of harmony

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:37.700 align:middle line:90%
we'd want to apply it to.

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:40.160 align:middle line:84%
Wanted to show you
another bit of Navajo

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.710 align:middle line:90%
before we leave Navajo behind.

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:46.940 align:middle line:84%
Here's how you say you
are playing in Navajo.

00:20:46.940 --> 00:20:48.547 align:middle line:90%
So we're switching tenses now.

00:20:48.547 --> 00:20:50.630 align:middle line:84%
We're going to get away
from sibilant and harmony.

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:54.800 align:middle line:84%
This is the present tense,
the imperfective aspect people

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:56.330 align:middle line:84%
call it when they
work on Navajo.

00:20:56.330 --> 00:20:57.378 align:middle line:90%
It's not exactly tense.

00:20:57.378 --> 00:20:59.420 align:middle line:84%
So this is how you say
you are playing in Navajo.

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:01.507 align:middle line:90%
It's [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:21:01.507 --> 00:21:04.720 align:middle line:90%


00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:08.020 align:middle line:84%
This verb has three
morphemes in it.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:10.960 align:middle line:84%
You can think of the "né" at
the end as the part that really

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:12.820 align:middle line:90%
means "play."

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:15.620 align:middle line:84%
There's a morpheme at
the beginning, "na-,"

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:18.580 align:middle line:90%
which is a prefix.

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:21.370 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to worry for
this about what it means.

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:23.560 align:middle line:84%
It's a marker of a
certain class of verbs

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:24.970 align:middle line:90%
what are called atelic verbs.

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:28.900 align:middle line:84%
These are verbs in which you
can continue doing something

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:32.210 align:middle line:84%
and there is no natural
endpoint to what you do.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:37.000 align:middle line:84%
So if you compare something
like walking around

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:39.460 align:middle line:90%
to walking to work--

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:44.300 align:middle line:84%
if you walk to work, then
when you get to work you stop.

00:21:44.300 --> 00:21:47.770 align:middle line:84%
And if you interrupt
me while I'm

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:49.270 align:middle line:84%
in the middle of
walking to work,

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:51.730 align:middle line:90%
then I did not walk to work.

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:56.770 align:middle line:84%
That's a telic predicate, as
opposed to walking around.

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:59.500 align:middle line:84%
If I'm walking around for an
hour and then you stop me,

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:01.780 align:middle line:84%
well, I still walked
around for a while.

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:02.860 align:middle line:90%
So that's atelic.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:06.160 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have a natural
endpoint, something

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:07.370 align:middle line:90%
you're trying to achieve.

00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:12.527 align:middle line:84%
So "na-" is a marker of atelic
verbs like play or walk around.

00:22:12.527 --> 00:22:14.360 align:middle line:84%
Feel free to ignore all
that if you want to.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.160 align:middle line:84%
So there's a "na-" at the
beginning of this verb.

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:18.220 align:middle line:84%
There's a "né" at the end, which
is the part that really means

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:18.730 align:middle line:90%
play.

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:20.280 align:middle line:84%
And then in between
them, there's

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:22.810 align:middle line:84%
an agreement morpheme,
something that's telling you

00:22:22.810 --> 00:22:24.460 align:middle line:90%
what the subject is.

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:29.080 align:middle line:84%
And that morpheme is "ni"
for you, and it's "sh" for I,

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:33.280 align:middle line:84%
and it's "oh" for you two,
and it's "ii'" for we two.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:38.366 align:middle line:84%
So you get
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]

00:22:38.366 --> 00:22:40.665 align:middle line:90%
for those forms of the verb.

00:22:40.665 --> 00:22:42.040 align:middle line:84%
So far, here's a
bunch of Navajo.

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:45.160 align:middle line:90%


00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:48.940 align:middle line:84%
Oh, one thing I have to ask you
to believe me about because it

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:51.840 align:middle line:84%
would be hard to demonstrate
is that the ee in we

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:55.030 align:middle line:84%
two are playing is underlying--
so that apostrophe there,

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:56.350 align:middle line:90%
this is Navajo orthography.

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:59.620 align:middle line:84%
It represents a glottal
stop, but that glottal stop

00:22:59.620 --> 00:23:00.910 align:middle line:90%
is originally a "d."

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:03.160 align:middle line:84%
I'll just ask you to
trust me about that

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:05.900 align:middle line:90%
for the next little while.

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:12.330 align:middle line:84%
Now I started with play, which
is a comparatively simple verb.

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:14.960 align:middle line:90%
The verb really is "né."

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:17.480 align:middle line:84%
But there are some other
verbs that we can talk about.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:21.560 align:middle line:84%
You've seen versions of them
in the preceding slides--

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:26.600 align:middle line:84%
work and investigate--
that I now want to turn to.

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:29.330 align:middle line:84%
Work and investigate--
we can think of them

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:31.340 align:middle line:84%
as starting with
clusters of consonants.

00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:33.380 align:middle line:90%
So you are working.

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:36.590 align:middle line:84%
You've got the "na" and the
"ni" that we have up above,

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:41.030 align:middle line:84%
and then the verb stem is
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:43.640 align:middle line:84%
People who work on Navajo
really think that that "l"

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:45.630 align:middle line:90%
is a separate morpheme.

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:48.650 align:middle line:90%
They call it the classifier.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:50.690 align:middle line:84%
Really, for our purposes,
all that amounts to

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:53.360 align:middle line:84%
is that there are a bunch of
verbs that start with "l,"

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:55.880 align:middle line:84%
and then the "l" is followed
by another consonant.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:58.920 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to worry too
much about what that is.

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:01.970 align:middle line:84%
So for our purposes, "working"
starts with two consonants,

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:02.990 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:24:02.990 --> 00:24:06.300 align:middle line:84%
And "investigating" also
starts with two consonants.

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:09.460 align:middle line:84%
The first one is a voiceless
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING] so this

00:24:09.460 --> 00:24:11.750 align:middle line:84%
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING] is
"You are investigating it."

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:15.430 align:middle line:90%


00:24:15.430 --> 00:24:16.890 align:middle line:90%
Why am I telling you all this?

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:20.140 align:middle line:90%
This is where the action is.

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:26.140 align:middle line:84%
"Ni-" is the prefix
for you singular,

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:28.390 align:middle line:90%
and it ends with a vowel.

00:24:28.390 --> 00:24:31.450 align:middle line:84%
But if we switch to the
other prefixes, which

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:35.630 align:middle line:84%
all end in consonants, then
some interesting things happen.

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:37.600 align:middle line:90%
So I am playing--

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:40.900 align:middle line:84%
we saw that the subject
agreement prefix for "I"

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:42.850 align:middle line:90%
is a "sh."

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:44.560 align:middle line:84%
So you get
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:46.460 align:middle line:84%
The subject agreement
prefix "sh,"

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:48.460 align:middle line:84%
if you put it before it
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING],,

00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:50.918 align:middle line:84%
you might have expected that
to be [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:24:50.918 --> 00:24:51.640 align:middle line:90%
But it isn't.

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:52.810 align:middle line:90%
It's [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:54.940 align:middle line:90%
So the "l" vanishes.

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:59.945 align:middle line:84%
Similarly for investigate,
we've decided that "investigate"

00:24:59.945 --> 00:25:01.300 align:middle line:90%
is [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:03.610 align:middle line:84%
So it starts with two
consonants, a voiceless "l"

00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:04.340 align:middle line:90%
and then a "k."

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:06.340 align:middle line:84%
If you also have a
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING] before

00:25:06.340 --> 00:25:08.440 align:middle line:84%
all that, well, you get
rid of the voiceless "l."

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:09.940 align:middle line:84%
So you get
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING] is

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:10.960 align:middle line:90%
"I'm investigating it."

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:16.980 align:middle line:90%


00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:19.950 align:middle line:84%
The reason I'm doing
these particular prefixes

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:22.720 align:middle line:84%
is that you can see different
things with the different ones.

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:28.620 align:middle line:84%
So if you add "oh," what happens
is that the "h" goes away,

00:25:28.620 --> 00:25:31.650 align:middle line:84%
but the distinction between
the voiced "l" in "work"

00:25:31.650 --> 00:25:34.750 align:middle line:84%
and the voiceless "l" in
"investigate" also goes away.

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:37.500 align:middle line:84%
So "work"-- we decided,
looking at you,

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:40.440 align:middle line:84%
starts with a voiced "l"
It's [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:42.990 align:middle line:84%
But "You two are working" is
now [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:46.110 align:middle line:84%
So the "h" is gone,
but that voiced

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:47.573 align:middle line:90%
"l" has become voiceless.

00:25:47.573 --> 00:25:48.240 align:middle line:90%
It's now barred.

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:50.517 align:middle line:90%


00:25:50.517 --> 00:25:52.350 align:middle line:84%
And we're getting a
voiceless "l" down there

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:53.933 align:middle line:84%
in "You two are
investigating it" too.

00:25:53.933 --> 00:25:56.390 align:middle line:90%
It's now [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:25:56.390 --> 00:26:02.400 align:middle line:84%
And the thing that I said
is underlyingly "eed," well,

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:04.557 align:middle line:84%
the "d" is gone,
but the distinction

00:26:04.557 --> 00:26:06.390 align:middle line:84%
between the voiced "l"
and the voiceless "l"

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:08.290 align:middle line:90%
has gone in the other direction.

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:09.630 align:middle line:90%
So now they're both voiced.

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:12.240 align:middle line:84%
So "We two are working" is now
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING] and "We

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:14.580 align:middle line:84%
two are investigating" is
now [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:16.622 align:middle line:84%
Do you remember when I
said that Navajo verbs are

00:26:16.622 --> 00:26:18.090 align:middle line:90%
extraordinarily complicated?

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:20.230 align:middle line:84%
Navajo verbs are
extraordinarily complicated.

00:26:20.230 --> 00:26:22.440 align:middle line:84%
There are all these
morphemes, but they

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:25.530 align:middle line:90%
don't coexist peacefully.

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:28.350 align:middle line:84%
When we were talking about
Native languages in which you

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:29.640 align:middle line:84%
have a whole bunch of
morphemes, and they're

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:30.990 align:middle line:84%
easy to separate
from each other,

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:32.790 align:middle line:84%
and there's nothing very
spectacular that happens,

00:26:32.790 --> 00:26:34.290 align:middle line:84%
you just attach
them to each other--

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:36.930 align:middle line:84%
this is the natural enemy
of that kind of language.

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:37.950 align:middle line:90%
It's the opposite.

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:39.727 align:middle line:84%
Here, we have a
bunch of morphemes,

00:26:39.727 --> 00:26:41.310 align:middle line:84%
and you put them
together, and there's

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:42.660 align:middle line:90%
like open warfare between them.

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:44.010 align:middle line:84%
There's all kinds
of strange stuff

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:45.927 align:middle line:84%
that happens as they
interact with each other.

00:26:45.927 --> 00:26:48.720 align:middle line:90%


00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:51.800 align:middle line:84%
So let me summarize
what we've just seen.

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:54.860 align:middle line:84%
If you have the "sh"
followed by two consonants,

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:57.590 align:middle line:84%
like if you're going to say "I
am investigating it," so "sh"

00:26:57.590 --> 00:27:00.148 align:middle line:84%
is the "I," and
"investigating," we decided,

00:27:00.148 --> 00:27:01.940 align:middle line:84%
starts with
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING] and then

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:06.930 align:middle line:84%
"k," you get rid of
the first consonant.

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:08.930 align:middle line:84%
So you get
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING] not

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:11.700 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:16.620 align:middle line:84%
If you have an "h"
followed by two consonants,

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:21.180 align:middle line:84%
like in "We two are working,"
so where you've got an "oh"

00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:23.850 align:middle line:84%
followed by
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING],, well,

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:27.600 align:middle line:84%
the "h" goes away,
but the "l" devoices.

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:30.270 align:middle line:84%
So instead of
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING],,

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:33.310 align:middle line:90%
we get [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING].

00:27:33.310 --> 00:27:38.130 align:middle line:84%
And if you have a "d"
before two consonants, well,

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:42.900 align:middle line:84%
the "d" goes away, but the "l"
that's in the first consonant

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:44.250 align:middle line:90%
will become voiced.

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:47.250 align:middle line:84%
So it changes from
[NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]----

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:51.402 align:middle line:84%
so "You two are investigating
it" to [NON-ENGLISH SPEAKING]..

00:27:51.402 --> 00:27:54.120 align:middle line:90%


00:27:54.120 --> 00:28:00.370 align:middle line:84%
Do these rules remind you of
anything other than the Navajo

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:03.370 align:middle line:90%
that we've just gone through?

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:07.950 align:middle line:84%
So we have one rule that says if
you have a "sh" followed by two

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:10.330 align:middle line:84%
consonants, get rid of
the first consonant,

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:13.350 align:middle line:84%
and another rule that says,
if you have an "h" followed

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:15.450 align:middle line:84%
by two consonants,
get rid of the "h"

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:18.680 align:middle line:84%
but devoice the first
consonant, and then another rule

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:21.680 align:middle line:84%
that says, if you have a "d"
followed by two consonants,

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:26.030 align:middle line:84%
get rid of the "d," oh, but
voice the first consonant.

00:28:26.030 --> 00:28:26.982 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:28:26.982 --> 00:28:28.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I might be
barking up the wrong tree,

00:28:28.940 --> 00:28:32.813 align:middle line:84%
but was there a language
that disallowed [INAUDIBLE]??

00:28:32.813 --> 00:28:34.230 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
There was indeed.

00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:35.772 align:middle line:84%
You're not barking
up the wrong tree.

00:28:35.772 --> 00:28:37.410 align:middle line:84%
You're barking up
the correct tree.

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:40.050 align:middle line:84%
It's not something I've ever had
to say before, but it's true.

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:42.340 align:middle line:84%
You are barking up
the Yawelmani tree.

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:44.910 align:middle line:84%
So we were talking
about Yawelmani before,

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:47.490 align:middle line:84%
and for Yawelmani, similarly,
we had a whole bunch

00:28:47.490 --> 00:28:49.890 align:middle line:84%
of phonological
phenomena, and we

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:52.230 align:middle line:84%
said we could write rules
to handle all of these facts

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:54.540 align:middle line:90%
and maybe we should.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:55.915 align:middle line:84%
In Yawelmani, it
was like, if you

00:28:55.915 --> 00:28:57.832 align:middle line:84%
have three consonants
in a row and one of them

00:28:57.832 --> 00:28:59.250 align:middle line:90%
is an "h," get rid of the "h."

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:00.720 align:middle line:84%
If you have three consonants
in a row and none of them

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:02.400 align:middle line:90%
is an "h," you insert a vowel.

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:04.110 align:middle line:84%
There are various
things that Yawelmani

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:06.390 align:middle line:84%
does to avoid having
three consonants in a row.

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:09.900 align:middle line:84%
Navajo is doing the same
thing, you're right--

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:12.120 align:middle line:84%
the same thing in the sense
that Navajo and Yawelmani

00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:15.060 align:middle line:84%
both dislike sequences of
three consonants in a row.

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:18.780 align:middle line:84%
None of these rules would
handle anything in Yawelmani.

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:20.820 align:middle line:84%
So this is not what
Yawelmani does to deal

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:22.448 align:middle line:90%
with three consonants in a row.

00:29:22.448 --> 00:29:23.865 align:middle line:84%
But if we're willing
to think of--

00:29:23.865 --> 00:29:26.560 align:middle line:90%


00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:28.560 align:middle line:84%
we're willing to think
of phonology the way I've

00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:31.080 align:middle line:84%
been advertising,
which is languages

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:33.030 align:middle line:84%
have certain problems,
and then they

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:34.950 align:middle line:84%
have means to deal
with the problems,

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:36.660 align:middle line:84%
then we can see
Yawelmani and Navajo

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:38.020 align:middle line:90%
as having something in common.

00:29:38.020 --> 00:29:40.270 align:middle line:84%
They have a kind of problem
that they both care about.

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:42.240 align:middle line:84%
They don't like sequences
of three consonants.

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:44.490 align:middle line:84%
They deal with it
in different ways,

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:47.680 align:middle line:84%
but we can see them as having
that property in common.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:48.180 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:49.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this
a property that's

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:50.815 align:middle line:84%
been observed in
other Indigenous

00:29:50.815 --> 00:29:52.895 align:middle line:84%
languages of the
Americas, or [INAUDIBLE]??

00:29:52.895 --> 00:29:54.270 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
I think it's not

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:56.580 align:middle line:84%
specific to Indigenous
languages of the Americas.

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:57.990 align:middle line:84%
I think it's
cross-linguistically

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:00.000 align:middle line:84%
not uncommon for
languages to dislike

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:03.360 align:middle line:90%
sequences of three consonants.

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:07.860 align:middle line:84%
And we talked a little bit
earlier on about the fact

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:12.510 align:middle line:84%
that some of your best cues
for the value of a consonant

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:15.840 align:middle line:84%
is its effects on
the nearby vowel.

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:20.070 align:middle line:84%
So I think we played
around with [INAUDIBLE],,

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:21.990 align:middle line:84%
showed you some
spectrograms from me saying

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:23.700 align:middle line:90%
"bab" and "dad" and "gag."

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:26.325 align:middle line:84%
I showed you that
during the stop itself,

00:30:26.325 --> 00:30:27.450 align:middle line:90%
you can't hear a whole lot.

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:30.690 align:middle line:84%
It's what the stop does to
the vowel that's nearby.

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:32.340 align:middle line:84%
If you have three
consonants in a row,

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:35.310 align:middle line:84%
then at least the one in the
middle is not next to a vowel,

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:37.680 align:middle line:84%
and so that could
be a reason why

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:39.720 align:middle line:84%
it's bad to have three
consonants in a row.

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:42.867 align:middle line:84%
It's perfectly
possible there are

00:30:42.867 --> 00:30:44.700 align:middle line:84%
languages out there
that are fine with three

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:45.540 align:middle line:90%
consonants in a row.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:47.170 align:middle line:84%
English is one of
them for example.

00:30:47.170 --> 00:30:50.160 align:middle line:84%
So we have words
like "strengths"--

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:52.320 align:middle line:90%
long strings of consonants.

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:53.820 align:middle line:84%
Plenty of other
languages like that.

00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:56.590 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, this is a kind
of thing that languages do.

00:30:56.590 --> 00:30:59.820 align:middle line:84%
And we're seeing it in
Yawelmani and Navajo.

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:02.760 align:middle line:84%
So this is one virtue
of the approach

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:05.010 align:middle line:84%
to phonology that I
was advertising before,

00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:08.795 align:middle line:84%
where we say, yeah, languages
have things they dislike,

00:31:08.795 --> 00:31:10.170 align:middle line:84%
and then, on the
other hand, they

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:12.810 align:middle line:84%
have techniques for dealing
with what they dislike.

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:14.550 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani and Navajo
have a property

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:16.440 align:middle line:84%
in common, namely the
property of disliking

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:17.610 align:middle line:90%
three consonants in a row.

00:31:17.610 --> 00:31:19.830 align:middle line:84%
They deal with that
problem in different ways,

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:22.420 align:middle line:84%
but they have that property
in common, at least.

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:24.237 align:middle line:84%
And so there's some
kind of insight

00:31:24.237 --> 00:31:26.070 align:middle line:84%
that we're gaining by
being willing to think

00:31:26.070 --> 00:31:27.537 align:middle line:90%
about phonology that way.

00:31:27.537 --> 00:31:29.370 align:middle line:84%
If we didn't think about
phonology that way,

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:33.810 align:middle line:84%
if we just made these
rules, Navajo and Yawelmani

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:36.900 align:middle line:84%
would seem not to have any
properties in common at all.

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:38.610 align:middle line:84%
We'd obscure that
fact about them.

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:39.330 align:middle line:90%
That make sense?

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:46.290 align:middle line:90%


00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:48.700 align:middle line:90%
So what have we done today--

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:51.180 align:middle line:84%
we've looked at a
variety of phenomena.

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:52.920 align:middle line:84%
I tried to introduce
you to the idea

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:54.900 align:middle line:84%
of autosegmental
representation, this idea

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:57.690 align:middle line:84%
that a single feature
can get spread

00:31:57.690 --> 00:31:59.880 align:middle line:84%
across different
places in a word,

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:04.500 align:middle line:84%
and we've just gone back to
talking about this idea of what

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:07.560 align:middle line:84%
are called ranked constraints,
this idea that languages might

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:13.088 align:middle line:84%
have a number of goals
in life, and the way

00:32:13.088 --> 00:32:14.880 align:middle line:84%
to talk about differences
between languages

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:17.340 align:middle line:84%
might just be in terms
of how they prioritize

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:18.340 align:middle line:90%
their different goals.

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:19.840 align:middle line:84%
So that was an
important part of how

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:22.260 align:middle line:84%
we talked about Finnish,
where the rules seem

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:25.170 align:middle line:84%
to be do vowel
harmony, oh, but don't

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:26.627 align:middle line:90%
create non-Finnish vowels.

00:32:26.627 --> 00:32:28.710 align:middle line:84%
So the most important thing
is don't create vowels

00:32:28.710 --> 00:32:31.560 align:middle line:84%
that Finnish doesn't have,
but modulo that, make

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:36.360 align:middle line:84%
all your vowels the same for
whether they're front or back.

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:38.328 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, we just
convinced ourselves

00:32:38.328 --> 00:32:40.620 align:middle line:84%
that Navajo and Yawelmani,
although they look different

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:43.140 align:middle line:84%
in lots of other ways, might
have a high-ranking constraint

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:47.340 align:middle line:84%
in common, namely, don't have
three consonants in a row.

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:51.180 align:middle line:84%
So what I want to
do now is show you

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:54.930 align:middle line:84%
another domain in which
people have fruitfully

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.960 align:middle line:84%
made use of this idea of
ranked constraints-- that is,

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:05.880 align:middle line:84%
of languages having a bunch of
comparatively simple conditions

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:09.960 align:middle line:84%
on what makes a word a
well-formed word that interact

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:12.180 align:middle line:84%
with each other
to give behaviors

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:14.220 align:middle line:90%
which can be quite complicated.

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:15.750 align:middle line:84%
And this all has
to do with stress.

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:18.000 align:middle line:84%
So we've already talked
some about stress.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:19.720 align:middle line:84%
It was part of
the flapping rule.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:21.660 align:middle line:84%
So when we were dealing
with how to deal

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:25.680 align:middle line:84%
with the difference in the
pronunciation of the "t"

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:28.710 align:middle line:84%
in these two words,
"atom" and "atomic,"

00:33:28.710 --> 00:33:32.310 align:middle line:84%
we said the rule that
converts "t" to a flap

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:34.590 align:middle line:84%
needs to be sensitive
to where stress

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:36.900 align:middle line:84%
is, so it only
happens after stress,

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:39.000 align:middle line:90%
doesn't happen before stress.

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:42.390 align:middle line:84%
And that's why we get it in
"atom" but not in "atomic."

00:33:42.390 --> 00:33:45.190 align:middle line:84%
Stress often has effects
on vowel quality.

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:47.370 align:middle line:84%
So if I were actually
writing "atom" and "atomic,"

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:48.990 align:middle line:84%
it would look
something like that.

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:54.660 align:middle line:84%
You can see that
"atom" and "atomic,"

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:57.690 align:middle line:84%
if we ignore the "-ic,"
"atom" and "atomic"

00:33:57.690 --> 00:34:00.930 align:middle line:84%
have almost nothing in
common apart from an "m."

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:05.100 align:middle line:84%
So the two vowels are
different and the sound

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:08.550 align:middle line:84%
that's spelled with a "t" is
different in the two words.

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:10.358 align:middle line:84%
I think I referred
to things like this

00:34:10.358 --> 00:34:12.150 align:middle line:84%
when I was talking
about the one good thing

00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:14.730 align:middle line:84%
about English spelling, which
is that a given morpheme is

00:34:14.730 --> 00:34:17.730 align:middle line:84%
typically spelled more or less
the same way, even if it's

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:19.335 align:middle line:90%
pronounced quite differently.

00:34:19.335 --> 00:34:23.300 align:middle line:90%


00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:26.630 align:middle line:84%
Stress is also important
for intonation.

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:28.820 align:middle line:84%
We won't have time
to talk seriously

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:31.130 align:middle line:90%
about intonation in this class.

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:33.530 align:middle line:84%
We probably won't have time
to talk about it at all.

00:34:33.530 --> 00:34:35.840 align:middle line:84%
Intonation is the study
of the rules for how

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:39.080 align:middle line:84%
the pitch of your voice
rises and falls as you speak,

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:41.570 align:middle line:84%
which, as you can imagine, is
a really complicated topic,

00:34:41.570 --> 00:34:43.969 align:middle line:90%
and it's really interesting.

00:34:43.969 --> 00:34:49.310 align:middle line:90%
One kind of thing for which--

00:34:49.310 --> 00:34:51.949 align:middle line:84%
here's a quick and
easy example of stress

00:34:51.949 --> 00:34:53.690 align:middle line:90%
being important for intonation.

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:56.756 align:middle line:90%
There's a kind of--

00:34:56.756 --> 00:34:59.450 align:middle line:90%
it's called evocative chant.

00:34:59.450 --> 00:35:01.670 align:middle line:84%
It's used in various
circumstances,

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:03.765 align:middle line:90%
but one is when you are calling.

00:35:03.765 --> 00:35:05.390 align:middle line:84%
I especially think
of it as when you're

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:07.557 align:middle line:84%
calling a child-- like
you're a parent trying to get

00:35:07.557 --> 00:35:09.710 align:middle line:90%
the children to come home.

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:14.000 align:middle line:84%
You say things like-- you
say their name and you say

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:16.040 align:middle line:90%
"Lau-ra."

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:17.495 align:middle line:90%
There's a tune to that.

00:35:17.495 --> 00:35:20.360 align:middle line:90%


00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:22.940 align:middle line:84%
If I were putting the tune
here, you might say yeah,

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:28.100 align:middle line:84%
there's a higher note, "Lau-,"
and a lower note, "-ra."

00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:30.340 align:middle line:84%
That's the way to
call Laura's name.

00:35:30.340 --> 00:35:32.090 align:middle line:84%
You use that when
you're calling children.

00:35:32.090 --> 00:35:34.225 align:middle line:84%
You use it when you're
selling hot dogs.

00:35:34.225 --> 00:35:35.600 align:middle line:84%
So if you're in
the ballpark, you

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:38.330 align:middle line:84%
say "hot dogs,
popcorn, peanuts."

00:35:38.330 --> 00:35:41.030 align:middle line:84%
There's this high-low
tune that you sing when

00:35:41.030 --> 00:35:43.940 align:middle line:90%
you're doing things like that.

00:35:43.940 --> 00:35:46.470 align:middle line:90%
How do you call Pierre?

00:35:46.470 --> 00:35:50.230 align:middle line:90%


00:35:50.230 --> 00:35:52.240 align:middle line:90%
"Pierre."

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:55.330 align:middle line:90%
It isn't "Pi-erre," yeah?

00:35:55.330 --> 00:35:58.030 align:middle line:90%
It's "Pierre."

00:35:58.030 --> 00:35:59.600 align:middle line:90%
So its high goes here.

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:01.630 align:middle line:90%
And then maybe also a low.

00:36:01.630 --> 00:36:04.090 align:middle line:84%
And over here there's,
I don't know, a low.

00:36:04.090 --> 00:36:06.920 align:middle line:90%
"Pierre."

00:36:06.920 --> 00:36:10.380 align:middle line:84%
What's the difference
between Laura and Pierre?

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:12.810 align:middle line:84%
I mean, Laura is a girl
and Pierre is a boy.

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:15.038 align:middle line:90%
But what else?

00:36:15.038 --> 00:36:16.830 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Stress is on
the end in "Pierre."

00:36:16.830 --> 00:36:18.720 align:middle line:84%
It's on the first
syllable with "Laura."

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:19.970 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, so the high--

00:36:19.970 --> 00:36:21.850 align:middle line:84%
we were just talking about
how you do the vocative chant.

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:23.805 align:middle line:84%
The high is on the
first syllable of Laura.

00:36:23.805 --> 00:36:27.935 align:middle line:84%
The high is on the second
syllable of Pierre.

00:36:27.935 --> 00:36:28.435 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:36:28.435 --> 00:36:31.440 align:middle line:90%


00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:37.130 align:middle line:84%
Let's chant some other
names, longer names.

00:36:37.130 --> 00:36:43.670 align:middle line:90%


00:36:43.670 --> 00:36:45.350 align:middle line:90%
Genevieve.

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:46.417 align:middle line:90%
"Genevieve."

00:36:46.417 --> 00:36:47.000 align:middle line:90%
Is that right?

00:36:47.000 --> 00:36:51.500 align:middle line:90%
"Genevieve," I think.

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:54.320 align:middle line:84%
Maybe this is the
low here at the end.

00:36:54.320 --> 00:36:56.020 align:middle line:90%
"Genevieve," hi ho.

00:36:56.020 --> 00:36:57.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:57.710 --> 00:37:03.290 align:middle line:84%
If you're selling
vegetables at the ballpark

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:04.295 align:middle line:90%
along with hot dogs--

00:37:04.295 --> 00:37:07.830 align:middle line:90%


00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:08.850 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Asparagus."

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:10.470 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"Asparagus," I think.

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:14.460 align:middle line:90%


00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:15.420 align:middle line:90%
This is low.

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:25.948 align:middle line:90%


00:37:25.948 --> 00:37:26.865 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Cucumbers."

00:37:26.865 --> 00:37:27.330 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Cu--"

00:37:27.330 --> 00:37:28.260 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "-cumbers."

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:30.450 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Cucumbers."

00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:33.180 align:middle line:84%
Think is it-- wait,
is it "cucumbers?"

00:37:33.180 --> 00:37:34.533 align:middle line:90%
Or is it "cucumbers"?

00:37:34.533 --> 00:37:35.825 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: No, that sounds good.

00:37:35.825 --> 00:37:37.785 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:37:37.785 --> 00:37:38.910 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Cucumbers.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:43.380 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so this is high
and this is low, Yeah?

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:46.270 align:middle line:90%


00:37:46.270 --> 00:37:48.270 align:middle line:90%
Let me do one more.

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:49.890 align:middle line:84%
Suppose you're
selling jalapenos.

00:37:49.890 --> 00:37:54.963 align:middle line:90%


00:37:54.963 --> 00:37:55.880 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Jalapenos."

00:37:55.880 --> 00:37:57.470 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Jalapenos."

00:37:57.470 --> 00:37:58.205 align:middle line:90%
Here's the high.

00:37:58.205 --> 00:38:01.352 align:middle line:90%


00:38:01.352 --> 00:38:02.810 align:middle line:84%
Forget about the
lows for a second.

00:38:02.810 --> 00:38:06.440 align:middle line:90%


00:38:06.440 --> 00:38:09.480 align:middle line:90%
Where does the high go?

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:09.980 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:38:09.980 --> 00:38:11.210 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The stressed syllable.

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:13.085 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: The
stressed syllable, yeah?

00:38:13.085 --> 00:38:18.410 align:middle line:84%
So it goes on "Genevieve,"
"Laura," "Pierre," yeah.

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:22.070 align:middle line:84%
"Cucumbers," "asparagus,"
and "jalapenos," that's

00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:23.972 align:middle line:90%
where the high starts, yeah.

00:38:23.972 --> 00:38:25.430 align:middle line:84%
And before that
high you have lows.

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:25.880 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:27.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There are some
situations where we keep

00:38:27.410 --> 00:38:28.550 align:middle line:90%
the high for a few syllables.

00:38:28.550 --> 00:38:29.090 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:38:29.090 --> 00:38:30.410 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: After the "s."

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:32.740 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, like where?

00:38:32.740 --> 00:38:34.742 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "Cu"--
wait, oh, "Genevieve."

00:38:34.742 --> 00:38:35.950 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Genevieve."

00:38:35.950 --> 00:38:37.100 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: And "asparagus."

00:38:37.100 --> 00:38:38.780 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
And "asparagus."

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:40.220 align:middle line:90%
No, "asparagus."

00:38:40.220 --> 00:38:42.740 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so for those you've
just get an "l" at the end

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:44.330 align:middle line:90%
and it's high until that.

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:47.000 align:middle line:84%
Maybe in all of these, you
get an "l" at the end, right?

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:49.580 align:middle line:84%
Even in Pierre, where
there's a high at the end,

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:50.570 align:middle line:90%
there's also a low.

00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:52.910 align:middle line:84%
I think you don't
say Pierre, right?

00:38:52.910 --> 00:38:54.410 align:middle line:90%
It's Pierre.

00:38:54.410 --> 00:38:56.270 align:middle line:90%
You have to go back down to low.

00:38:56.270 --> 00:39:00.260 align:middle line:84%
So the vocative chant goes put
a high on the stressed syllable,

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:02.600 align:middle line:90%
put a low at the end, yeah?

00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:06.680 align:middle line:84%
And then what's the
difference between "cucumbers"

00:39:06.680 --> 00:39:07.790 align:middle line:90%
and "asparagus"?

00:39:07.790 --> 00:39:12.520 align:middle line:90%


00:39:12.520 --> 00:39:18.080 align:middle line:84%
Or between "Genevieve"--
wait, do we want "Genevieve"?

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:19.330 align:middle line:90%
No, we don't want "Genevieve."

00:39:19.330 --> 00:39:21.170 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, Genevieve.

00:39:21.170 --> 00:39:22.420 align:middle line:90%
My sister's name is Genevieve.

00:39:22.420 --> 00:39:23.795 align:middle line:84%
Nobody tell her
that I said that.

00:39:23.795 --> 00:39:27.500 align:middle line:90%


00:39:27.500 --> 00:39:30.190 align:middle line:84%
What's the difference between
"cucumbers" and "asparagus"?

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:31.990 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:39:31.990 --> 00:39:36.071 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, we haven't
learned syllable division yet.

00:39:36.071 --> 00:39:36.988 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:39:36.988 --> 00:39:39.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Singing
"cucumbers" added

00:39:39.610 --> 00:39:41.548 align:middle line:90%
part of that second syllable.

00:39:41.548 --> 00:39:43.590 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep, yep,
that's probably right.

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:46.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Whereas in
"Genevieve" that is not

00:39:46.320 --> 00:39:47.727 align:middle line:90%
part of the second syllable.

00:39:47.727 --> 00:39:48.644 align:middle line:90%
Same with "asparagus."

00:39:48.644 --> 00:39:49.644 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Mm-hmm.

00:39:49.644 --> 00:39:50.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:39:50.670 --> 00:39:52.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's probably right.

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:54.180 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, yeah, that's
probably true.

00:39:54.180 --> 00:39:55.095 align:middle line:90%
Does it?

00:39:55.095 --> 00:39:56.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We have
some data like you're

00:39:56.720 --> 00:39:58.790 align:middle line:90%
high until you hit an obstruent.

00:39:58.790 --> 00:39:59.440 align:middle line:90%
And then--

00:39:59.440 --> 00:40:00.732 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ooh, oh, gosh.

00:40:00.732 --> 00:40:03.050 align:middle line:90%


00:40:03.050 --> 00:40:05.057 align:middle line:84%
You're high until
you hit an obstruent.

00:40:05.057 --> 00:40:06.515 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So low
until they stress.

00:40:06.515 --> 00:40:07.340 align:middle line:90%
And then--

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:08.950 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, man.

00:40:08.950 --> 00:40:10.700 align:middle line:84%
Let me try to think
of some more examples.

00:40:10.700 --> 00:40:12.830 align:middle line:84%
That can't possibly be
right, but it covers the data

00:40:12.830 --> 00:40:13.747 align:middle line:90%
that we've got so far.

00:40:13.747 --> 00:40:15.885 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:40:15.885 --> 00:40:16.385 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:40:16.385 --> 00:40:16.820 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The last one.

00:40:16.820 --> 00:40:18.080 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: The last
one has to be low, yeah.

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:19.430 align:middle line:90%
So the last one has to be low.

00:40:19.430 --> 00:40:20.120 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:40:20.120 --> 00:40:22.760 align:middle line:84%
And the high goes on
the stressed syllable.

00:40:22.760 --> 00:40:26.725 align:middle line:84%
And then you stay
high maybe, so far.

00:40:26.725 --> 00:40:28.100 align:middle line:84%
For most of these
what happens is

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:30.350 align:middle line:84%
that you stay high until
you hit a low, right?

00:40:30.350 --> 00:40:31.640 align:middle line:90%
So it's "Genevieve."

00:40:31.640 --> 00:40:34.340 align:middle line:84%
The "vieve" is at the
end, or it's "asparagus"

00:40:34.340 --> 00:40:35.610 align:middle line:90%
or it's "jalapenos."

00:40:35.610 --> 00:40:38.810 align:middle line:90%


00:40:38.810 --> 00:40:40.410 align:middle line:90%
And these are low.

00:40:40.410 --> 00:40:42.372 align:middle line:84%
So you're low before
you get to the high.

00:40:42.372 --> 00:40:44.330 align:middle line:84%
And the real question
is, what's the difference

00:40:44.330 --> 00:40:46.310 align:middle line:84%
between "cucumbers"
and "asparagus,"

00:40:46.310 --> 00:40:49.220 align:middle line:84%
where this high spreads and
this high just stays here?

00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:52.070 align:middle line:84%
Why is this low not just--
why is there a low not just

00:40:52.070 --> 00:40:54.110 align:middle line:90%
here, but also here?

00:40:54.110 --> 00:40:55.550 align:middle line:90%
And I think that's true, right?

00:40:55.550 --> 00:40:57.800 align:middle line:84%
We agreed that it
is "cucumbers."

00:40:57.800 --> 00:40:59.060 align:middle line:90%
It isn't "cucumbers."

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:04.710 align:middle line:90%


00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:07.074 align:middle line:84%
Let me try to think
of another word.

00:41:07.074 --> 00:41:08.370 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Endocrinology."

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:09.750 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"Endocrinology."

00:41:09.750 --> 00:41:14.970 align:middle line:90%


00:41:14.970 --> 00:41:15.870 align:middle line:90%
What's a better word?

00:41:15.870 --> 00:41:16.710 align:middle line:90%
That's a good word.

00:41:16.710 --> 00:41:18.703 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:41:18.703 --> 00:41:20.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to think
of another word that

00:41:20.370 --> 00:41:22.635 align:middle line:84%
has the property
that I'm looking for.

00:41:22.635 --> 00:41:25.870 align:middle line:90%


00:41:25.870 --> 00:41:28.630 align:middle line:90%
Lend a, "interdisciplinary."

00:41:28.630 --> 00:41:30.130 align:middle line:90%
"Interdisciplinary.

00:41:30.130 --> 00:41:31.130 align:middle line:90%
Interdisciplinary.

00:41:31.130 --> 00:41:33.750 align:middle line:90%
Interdisciplinary."

00:41:33.750 --> 00:41:36.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, no.

00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:37.080 align:middle line:90%
"Interdisciplinary."

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:39.060 align:middle line:84%
So suppose I'm selling
interdisciplinary.

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:40.410 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:40.410 --> 00:41:43.950 align:middle line:84%
Or suppose I've named my
child "Interdisciplinary."

00:41:43.950 --> 00:41:48.010 align:middle line:84%
It's "Interdisciplinary,"
is that right?

00:41:48.010 --> 00:41:51.660 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I think that's right.

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:56.430 align:middle line:84%
So my-- that's a nice
name for a child.

00:41:56.430 --> 00:42:02.430 align:middle line:90%
I mean, "Interdisciplinary."

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:04.290 align:middle line:90%
It's low here, yeah?

00:42:04.290 --> 00:42:08.140 align:middle line:90%


00:42:08.140 --> 00:42:12.280 align:middle line:84%
Man, so let me tell you
what I think is going on.

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:14.530 align:middle line:84%
We've talked about words
as though they only

00:42:14.530 --> 00:42:20.080 align:middle line:84%
have one stress, "cucumber,"
"asparagus," "Genevieve."

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:22.420 align:middle line:90%
Let's take "interdisciplinary."

00:42:22.420 --> 00:42:24.700 align:middle line:84%
How many stresses are there
in "interdisciplinary"?

00:42:24.700 --> 00:42:26.860 align:middle line:84%
Where's the main stress
in "interdisciplinary"?

00:42:26.860 --> 00:42:28.120 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's "dis" here.

00:42:28.120 --> 00:42:29.120 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, it's "dis,"

00:42:29.120 --> 00:42:30.460 align:middle line:90%
it's here, where the high is.

00:42:30.460 --> 00:42:31.960 align:middle line:84%
So the high goes
on the main stress.

00:42:31.960 --> 00:42:34.510 align:middle line:84%
What other syllables
in this are stressed?

00:42:34.510 --> 00:42:35.268 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "-nary."

00:42:35.268 --> 00:42:36.310 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "-nary."

00:42:36.310 --> 00:42:37.930 align:middle line:90%
There's another stress.

00:42:37.930 --> 00:42:40.030 align:middle line:90%
And maybe here, I don't know.

00:42:40.030 --> 00:42:41.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Really, OK.

00:42:41.530 --> 00:42:43.348 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:42:43.348 --> 00:42:45.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this high
on the main stress

00:42:45.550 --> 00:42:47.320 align:middle line:84%
and then it stays
high until it reaches

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:48.730 align:middle line:90%
the next like substress?

00:42:48.730 --> 00:42:50.120 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I think
it's something like that.

00:42:50.120 --> 00:42:51.880 align:middle line:84%
So it's high and it
stays high until you get

00:42:51.880 --> 00:42:53.140 align:middle line:90%
to the next stressed syllable.

00:42:53.140 --> 00:42:55.460 align:middle line:84%
The next stressed syllable
is where the low starts.

00:42:55.460 --> 00:42:55.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:42:55.960 --> 00:42:58.880 align:middle line:84%
And there's always
a low at the end.

00:42:58.880 --> 00:43:01.840 align:middle line:84%
So for the difference between
"cucumbers" and "asparagus,"

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:04.450 align:middle line:84%
I think, is that "cucumber"
has a secondary stress

00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:08.560 align:middle line:84%
on the second syllable,
whereas "asparagus" only has

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:09.100 align:middle line:90%
one stress.

00:43:09.100 --> 00:43:09.693 align:middle line:90%
It's here.

00:43:09.693 --> 00:43:11.860 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and that might be
related to your observation.

00:43:11.860 --> 00:43:14.830 align:middle line:84%
This has a closed syllable here
and closed syllables are often

00:43:14.830 --> 00:43:17.450 align:middle line:90%
associated with stress.

00:43:17.450 --> 00:43:18.980 align:middle line:84%
This will not be
on the final exam.

00:43:18.980 --> 00:43:20.000 align:middle line:90%
There is no final exam.

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:20.500 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:24.190 align:middle line:84%
But here's an example
of stress being useful

00:43:24.190 --> 00:43:27.070 align:middle line:84%
for understanding a phenomenon,
in this case, the phenomenon

00:43:27.070 --> 00:43:29.283 align:middle line:90%
of the vocative chant.

00:43:29.283 --> 00:43:30.700 align:middle line:84%
All of you are
about to be invited

00:43:30.700 --> 00:43:34.120 align:middle line:84%
to come up with a research
topic, something to work on.

00:43:34.120 --> 00:43:37.840 align:middle line:84%
This is something you could
try working on with the person

00:43:37.840 --> 00:43:39.430 align:middle line:90%
that you're working with.

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:41.860 align:middle line:84%
Ask them how they call
their children, right?

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:43.450 align:middle line:90%
And also how they sell things.

00:43:43.450 --> 00:43:45.400 align:middle line:84%
And find out whether
there's a special tune

00:43:45.400 --> 00:43:48.910 align:middle line:84%
for that, because in
many languages there is.

00:43:48.910 --> 00:43:51.560 align:middle line:84%
It would be interesting
to see, yeah.

00:43:51.560 --> 00:43:53.200 align:middle line:90%
OK, all right.

00:43:53.200 --> 00:43:56.830 align:middle line:90%


00:43:56.830 --> 00:43:59.092 align:middle line:84%
So, yeah, this
was all an attempt

00:43:59.092 --> 00:44:01.300 align:middle line:84%
to convince you that it
might be interesting to think

00:44:01.300 --> 00:44:02.110 align:middle line:90%
about stress.

00:44:02.110 --> 00:44:05.020 align:middle line:84%
It's relevant for various types
of phonological phenomenon.

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:07.930 align:middle line:84%
It was relevant for the
flapping rule in English.

00:44:07.930 --> 00:44:11.110 align:middle line:84%
We've just seen it's
relevant for the rules

00:44:11.110 --> 00:44:12.130 align:middle line:90%
for vocative chant.

00:44:12.130 --> 00:44:15.128 align:middle line:90%


00:44:15.128 --> 00:44:16.670 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to leave
those on the board

00:44:16.670 --> 00:44:18.560 align:middle line:84%
just so future
classes can wonder

00:44:18.560 --> 00:44:21.290 align:middle line:90%
what the heck we were doing.

00:44:21.290 --> 00:44:26.228 align:middle line:84%
In languages where words have
one fixed position for stress--

00:44:26.228 --> 00:44:28.770 align:middle line:84%
so there are languages out there
where the position of stress

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:30.390 align:middle line:90%
is always fixed.

00:44:30.390 --> 00:44:32.030 align:middle line:84%
These are data from
a database called

00:44:32.030 --> 00:44:36.200 align:middle line:84%
WALS, the world atlas
of language structures,

00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:38.930 align:middle line:90%
which is free and accessible.

00:44:38.930 --> 00:44:42.680 align:middle line:84%
I recommend going and
having a look at it.

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:46.970 align:middle line:84%
When I got these data,
there were 282 languages

00:44:46.970 --> 00:44:48.620 align:middle line:90%
described in the WALS database.

00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:50.900 align:middle line:84%
The WALS database describes
languages with respect

00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:55.040 align:middle line:84%
to a variety of phenomena,
not always accurately, I

00:44:55.040 --> 00:44:55.650 align:middle line:90%
have to say.

00:44:55.650 --> 00:44:58.280 align:middle line:84%
So if you have a particular
language you're interested in,

00:44:58.280 --> 00:44:59.280 align:middle line:90%
you should double check.

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:03.560 align:middle line:84%
But it's a nice first pass,
looking at a bunch of data.

00:45:03.560 --> 00:45:08.270 align:middle line:84%
In that database, you can see
there are, among languages

00:45:08.270 --> 00:45:12.230 align:middle line:84%
that have just one place
where stress always goes,

00:45:12.230 --> 00:45:15.143 align:middle line:84%
there are languages
where that one

00:45:15.143 --> 00:45:16.310 align:middle line:90%
place is the first syllable.

00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:17.450 align:middle line:90%
That's pretty common.

00:45:17.450 --> 00:45:20.970 align:middle line:84%
About a third of the languages
like that, that do that.

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:25.460 align:middle line:84%
The most common type of stress
for a language like that

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:26.780 align:middle line:90%
is penultimate stress.

00:45:26.780 --> 00:45:29.570 align:middle line:84%
Languages like Zulu and
Polish have stress on the next

00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:31.670 align:middle line:90%
to last syllable.

00:45:31.670 --> 00:45:34.430 align:middle line:84%
And then you can see there
are some other patterns that

00:45:34.430 --> 00:45:35.250 align:middle line:90%
are less common.

00:45:35.250 --> 00:45:38.940 align:middle line:84%
So the last syllable
is not uncommon.

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:41.270 align:middle line:84%
And then there are some
really weird systems

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:44.630 align:middle line:84%
like Hocak, which is a
Native American language,

00:45:44.630 --> 00:45:46.760 align:middle line:84%
has stress on its
third syllable.

00:45:46.760 --> 00:45:47.662 align:middle line:90%
That's quite rare.

00:45:47.662 --> 00:45:49.370 align:middle line:84%
There are some languages
that have stress

00:45:49.370 --> 00:45:54.952 align:middle line:84%
on the antepenultimate
syllable, antepenultimate, yeah,

00:45:54.952 --> 00:45:57.410 align:middle line:84%
and some languages that have
stress on the second syllable.

00:45:57.410 --> 00:45:59.713 align:middle line:84%
So these are all
patterns that you find.

00:45:59.713 --> 00:46:01.130 align:middle line:84%
As you can see,
these are patterns

00:46:01.130 --> 00:46:06.590 align:middle line:84%
that are best described as
stressing a syllable that

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:12.710 align:middle line:84%
is counted either from the left
edge or the right of the word.

00:46:12.710 --> 00:46:16.070 align:middle line:84%
There are other systems
that are not hard to imagine

00:46:16.070 --> 00:46:17.340 align:middle line:90%
but that don't exist.

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:21.830 align:middle line:84%
And so people who are developing
theories of stress typology,

00:46:21.830 --> 00:46:23.690 align:middle line:84%
one of their goals,
for example, is

00:46:23.690 --> 00:46:26.690 align:middle line:84%
to have a theory that rules
out languages with the rule,

00:46:26.690 --> 00:46:28.220 align:middle line:84%
stress the syllable
that's closest

00:46:28.220 --> 00:46:30.020 align:middle line:90%
to the middle of the word.

00:46:30.020 --> 00:46:32.210 align:middle line:84%
It's distressingly
easy to come up

00:46:32.210 --> 00:46:34.520 align:middle line:84%
with a theory that predicts
the existence of languages

00:46:34.520 --> 00:46:35.030 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:46:35.030 --> 00:46:36.720 align:middle line:90%
And they don't exist.

00:46:36.720 --> 00:46:42.900 align:middle line:84%
So you want your theory to not
capture languages like that.

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:46.760 align:middle line:84%
One way to describe this kind of
pattern is to have constraints,

00:46:46.760 --> 00:46:48.740 align:middle line:84%
you know, like the
constraints that say things

00:46:48.740 --> 00:46:51.650 align:middle line:84%
like don't have three consonants
in a row, constraints that

00:46:51.650 --> 00:46:55.190 align:middle line:84%
say things like put stress
as far to the left or as far

00:46:55.190 --> 00:46:57.690 align:middle line:84%
to the right in the
word as possible.

00:46:57.690 --> 00:47:02.840 align:middle line:84%
And then have that constraint
interact with other constraints

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:05.480 align:middle line:84%
that say things like don't
stress the last syllable

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:08.180 align:middle line:84%
or don't stress
the first syllable.

00:47:08.180 --> 00:47:10.950 align:middle line:84%
So for example, the
quite common pattern

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:12.950 align:middle line:84%
that you get in Zulu and
Polish where you stress

00:47:12.950 --> 00:47:15.170 align:middle line:90%
the next to last syllable.

00:47:15.170 --> 00:47:17.540 align:middle line:84%
That's one that says
put stress as far

00:47:17.540 --> 00:47:19.340 align:middle line:84%
to the right in the
word as you can, oh,

00:47:19.340 --> 00:47:21.262 align:middle line:84%
but don't stress
the last syllable.

00:47:21.262 --> 00:47:22.970 align:middle line:84%
So don't stress the
last syllable, that's

00:47:22.970 --> 00:47:24.080 align:middle line:90%
the most important thing.

00:47:24.080 --> 00:47:27.570 align:middle line:84%
But putting aside that, put
stress as far to the right

00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:28.070 align:middle line:90%
as you can.

00:47:28.070 --> 00:47:30.477 align:middle line:84%
That means put stress on
the next-to-last syllable.

00:47:30.477 --> 00:47:32.810 align:middle line:84%
That's one way of describing
that kind of stress system.

00:47:32.810 --> 00:47:33.530 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:47:33.530 --> 00:47:34.160 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:47:34.160 --> 00:47:35.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are these
languages sensitive to

00:47:35.530 --> 00:47:37.488 align:middle line:84%
deal with a verb which
comes in the last place,

00:47:37.488 --> 00:47:39.160 align:middle line:84%
anyway it felt like
it was for that.

00:47:39.160 --> 00:47:40.900 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
so there are languages--

00:47:40.900 --> 00:47:42.970 align:middle line:84%
we looked at this in
Lardil, that just don't like

00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:44.140 align:middle line:90%
words of only one syllable.

00:47:44.140 --> 00:47:46.093 align:middle line:90%
They rule them out.

00:47:46.093 --> 00:47:47.260 align:middle line:90%
But you're absolutely right.

00:47:47.260 --> 00:47:52.060 align:middle line:84%
So languages that have systems
like these, typically it's like

00:47:52.060 --> 00:47:55.250 align:middle line:84%
stress the next-to-last
syllable if there is one.

00:47:55.250 --> 00:47:58.510 align:middle line:84%
So in one monosyllabic word,
you stress the one syllable

00:47:58.510 --> 00:47:59.210 align:middle line:90%
that you've got.

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:01.060 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a very good point.

00:48:01.060 --> 00:48:04.140 align:middle line:84%
That's a thing that you
find in these languages.

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:08.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:14.190 align:middle line:84%
It would be nice if there were
some correlation between having

00:48:14.190 --> 00:48:19.950 align:middle line:84%
a stress system that wanted to
avoid final stress, let's say,

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:25.143 align:middle line:84%
and being like Lardil in not
liking monosyllables, right?

00:48:25.143 --> 00:48:27.060 align:middle line:84%
It would be nice if the
world worked that way.

00:48:27.060 --> 00:48:28.620 align:middle line:90%
As far as I know it doesn't.

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:31.695 align:middle line:84%
So it doesn't seem as though
the condition that says it

00:48:31.695 --> 00:48:33.570 align:middle line:84%
would be better for your
words to be at least

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:36.240 align:middle line:84%
two syllables long, it
doesn't seem to be motivated

00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:37.768 align:middle line:90%
by these considerations.

00:48:37.768 --> 00:48:39.810 align:middle line:84%
I sure wish that it was,
because it would be nice

00:48:39.810 --> 00:48:42.960 align:middle line:84%
if there-- then we would have
an explanation for part of that.

00:48:42.960 --> 00:48:45.240 align:middle line:84%
But it's not clear to me
that there is any connection

00:48:45.240 --> 00:48:48.170 align:middle line:90%
between those things.

00:48:48.170 --> 00:48:48.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:48:48.670 --> 00:48:51.600 align:middle line:90%


00:48:51.600 --> 00:48:55.050 align:middle line:84%
Now those, we were just talking
about languages in which stress

00:48:55.050 --> 00:48:56.490 align:middle line:90%
is only in one place.

00:48:56.490 --> 00:48:58.590 align:middle line:84%
But as we actually
saw as we were looking

00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:00.758 align:middle line:84%
at the vocative chant, it's
possible for a language

00:49:00.758 --> 00:49:02.550 align:middle line:84%
to have multiple stresses
in a single word.

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:04.570 align:middle line:90%
English does that all the time.

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:06.660 align:middle line:84%
And there are
languages out there

00:49:06.660 --> 00:49:11.370 align:middle line:84%
that have patterns where you
stress more than one syllable.

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:13.440 align:middle line:84%
Here are some
examples from Pintupi,

00:49:13.440 --> 00:49:16.350 align:middle line:84%
which is an Aboriginal
language of Australia,

00:49:16.350 --> 00:49:20.490 align:middle line:84%
in which the basic
rule is stress

00:49:20.490 --> 00:49:22.050 align:middle line:90%
the odd-numbered syllables.

00:49:22.050 --> 00:49:25.380 align:middle line:84%
So stress, put stress on the
first, third, fifth, seventh,

00:49:25.380 --> 00:49:29.340 align:middle line:84%
and et cetera,
syllables of the word.

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:33.140 align:middle line:84%
So again, you can think
of that as a little pile

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:34.070 align:middle line:90%
of constraints.

00:49:34.070 --> 00:49:37.490 align:middle line:84%
Stress the first syllable,
don't stress the last syllable,

00:49:37.490 --> 00:49:41.030 align:middle line:84%
and don't allow yourself to
have two syllables in a row that

00:49:41.030 --> 00:49:46.130 align:middle line:84%
are the same, that is are both
stressed or both unstressed.

00:49:46.130 --> 00:49:49.340 align:middle line:84%
So, again, you
don't always succeed

00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:51.170 align:middle line:90%
in doing all of these things.

00:49:51.170 --> 00:49:54.710 align:middle line:84%
In a language like
this, whenever you have

00:49:54.710 --> 00:49:58.820 align:middle line:84%
an odd-numbered of
syllables in your word,

00:49:58.820 --> 00:50:01.190 align:middle line:90%
you can achieve the first two--

00:50:01.190 --> 00:50:04.940 align:middle line:84%
stress the first syllable,
don't stress the last syllable--

00:50:04.940 --> 00:50:06.980 align:middle line:84%
and actually the next
one, don't allow two

00:50:06.980 --> 00:50:08.990 align:middle line:90%
stressed syllables in a row.

00:50:08.990 --> 00:50:11.420 align:middle line:84%
But the last one, don't allow
two unstressed syllables

00:50:11.420 --> 00:50:16.100 align:middle line:84%
in a row, you achieve that
in the words that have

00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:17.367 align:middle line:90%
an even number of syllables.

00:50:17.367 --> 00:50:18.950 align:middle line:84%
No, yeah, an even
number of syllables,

00:50:18.950 --> 00:50:20.750 align:middle line:84%
but not the ones that have
an odd number of syllables.

00:50:20.750 --> 00:50:21.920 align:middle line:84%
If there's an odd
number of syllables,

00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:24.212 align:middle line:84%
like in the second word there
that has three syllables,

00:50:24.212 --> 00:50:25.700 align:middle line:84%
you only stress
the first syllable.

00:50:25.700 --> 00:50:27.350 align:middle line:84%
And you put up with
the fact that it

00:50:27.350 --> 00:50:29.000 align:middle line:84%
ends in two
unstressed syllables,

00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:33.080 align:middle line:84%
because there's no way to avoid
that while also satisfying

00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:34.670 align:middle line:84%
these higher-ranking
constraints like

00:50:34.670 --> 00:50:36.873 align:middle line:84%
don't stress the last
syllable and don't have two

00:50:36.873 --> 00:50:38.040 align:middle line:90%
stressed syllables in a row.

00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:38.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:50:38.900 --> 00:50:42.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How come we go through
these four different rules,

00:50:42.080 --> 00:50:46.142 align:middle line:84%
as opposed to just declaring
stress every other syllable?

00:50:46.142 --> 00:50:48.350 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Stress
every other syllable starting

00:50:48.350 --> 00:50:50.240 align:middle line:90%
from the left edge, yeah.

00:50:50.240 --> 00:50:51.860 align:middle line:84%
Why am I trying
to decompose this

00:50:51.860 --> 00:50:53.990 align:middle line:90%
into constraints like this?

00:50:53.990 --> 00:50:57.920 align:middle line:84%
I guess in part it's
an attempt to constrain

00:50:57.920 --> 00:51:01.470 align:middle line:84%
the kinds of syllable, of stress
systems that we could find,

00:51:01.470 --> 00:51:01.970 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:04.910 align:middle line:84%
So if we let ourselves
say things like stress

00:51:04.910 --> 00:51:06.655 align:middle line:90%
the odd-numbered syllables--

00:51:06.655 --> 00:51:08.780 align:middle line:84%
I mean first, stress the
odd-number of syllables by

00:51:08.780 --> 00:51:10.610 align:middle line:84%
itself doesn't
actually cover this,

00:51:10.610 --> 00:51:12.290 align:middle line:90%
even though it's what I said.

00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:15.350 align:middle line:84%
So it's really stress the
odd-numbered syllables

00:51:15.350 --> 00:51:17.480 align:middle line:84%
unless you're going to
stress the last syllable.

00:51:17.480 --> 00:51:18.800 align:middle line:90%
Don't stress the last syllable.

00:51:18.800 --> 00:51:22.610 align:middle line:84%
So even just saying
that, what I just said

00:51:22.610 --> 00:51:25.640 align:middle line:84%
is best stated in terms of
conflicting constraints.

00:51:25.640 --> 00:51:27.318 align:middle line:84%
It's like the most
important thing is

00:51:27.318 --> 00:51:28.610 align:middle line:90%
don't stress the last syllable.

00:51:28.610 --> 00:51:30.440 align:middle line:84%
And then underneath
that there's stress

00:51:30.440 --> 00:51:32.300 align:middle line:90%
the odd-numbered syllables.

00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:35.990 align:middle line:84%
And then, by decomposing stress
the odd-numbered syllables

00:51:35.990 --> 00:51:39.098 align:middle line:84%
into stress the
first syllable, don't

00:51:39.098 --> 00:51:40.640 align:middle line:84%
have two stressed
syllables in a row,

00:51:40.640 --> 00:51:43.460 align:middle line:84%
don't have two unstressed
syllables in a row,

00:51:43.460 --> 00:51:47.360 align:middle line:84%
we constrain the kinds of stress
systems that we could find.

00:51:47.360 --> 00:51:49.370 align:middle line:84%
So there aren't
stress systems that

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:52.760 align:middle line:84%
say stress the prime
numbered syllables

00:51:52.760 --> 00:51:55.827 align:middle line:84%
or stress the
powers of 2, right?

00:51:55.827 --> 00:51:57.410 align:middle line:84%
There are certain
kinds of constraints

00:51:57.410 --> 00:51:59.450 align:middle line:84%
that you use for stress
systems and not others.

00:51:59.450 --> 00:52:02.030 align:middle line:84%
And it's one of the kinds of
things we're trying to get.

00:52:02.030 --> 00:52:03.373 align:middle line:90%
A good question.

00:52:03.373 --> 00:52:04.040 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:05.330 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:52:05.330 --> 00:52:08.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How far would
you split into three rules

00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:09.320 align:middle line:90%
here, right?

00:52:09.320 --> 00:52:09.770 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:52:09.770 --> 00:52:11.273 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: First of all
going around the two

00:52:11.273 --> 00:52:13.856 align:middle line:84%
stressed syllables in a row, so
I'm not sure on the second one

00:52:13.856 --> 00:52:14.690 align:middle line:90%
now.

00:52:14.690 --> 00:52:15.240 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:17.115 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And don't
stress the last syllable,

00:52:17.115 --> 00:52:18.710 align:middle line:84%
like going to the
three dot three.

00:52:18.710 --> 00:52:21.710 align:middle line:84%
So I dictate, my mind
goes very close somewhere.

00:52:21.710 --> 00:52:24.680 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes, yes,
yes, I see what you mean.

00:52:24.680 --> 00:52:27.680 align:middle line:84%
So we could ask ourselves,
what would happen if you had--

00:52:27.680 --> 00:52:29.420 align:middle line:84%
and this is either
a virtue or a flaw

00:52:29.420 --> 00:52:31.010 align:middle line:84%
of this way of
talking about it, what

00:52:31.010 --> 00:52:33.560 align:middle line:84%
would happen if the second
thing there, don't stress

00:52:33.560 --> 00:52:36.890 align:middle line:84%
the last syllable, were
the first constraint,

00:52:36.890 --> 00:52:38.383 align:middle line:84%
or if it were the
third constraint,

00:52:38.383 --> 00:52:39.800 align:middle line:84%
or if it were the
last constraint.

00:52:39.800 --> 00:52:41.967 align:middle line:84%
We get to ask ourselves,
do we get different systems

00:52:41.967 --> 00:52:43.708 align:middle line:84%
if we mess with the
constraints that way?

00:52:43.708 --> 00:52:45.500 align:middle line:84%
And this is indeed the
kind of thing people

00:52:45.500 --> 00:52:48.480 align:middle line:84%
do when they're doing this
kind of work, you know.

00:52:48.480 --> 00:52:52.930 align:middle line:90%


00:52:52.930 --> 00:52:54.860 align:middle line:90%
Here's another stress system.

00:52:54.860 --> 00:52:59.650 align:middle line:84%
This is Passamaquoddy,
in which the rules

00:52:59.650 --> 00:53:04.870 align:middle line:84%
go, stress the first syllable,
don't stress the last syllable.

00:53:04.870 --> 00:53:07.210 align:middle line:84%
Those are the most
important things.

00:53:07.210 --> 00:53:10.690 align:middle line:84%
You can see that those are
true in all of these examples.

00:53:10.690 --> 00:53:16.270 align:middle line:84%
And don't allow two
unstressed syllables in a row.

00:53:16.270 --> 00:53:18.310 align:middle line:84%
And then down at the
bottom of the list

00:53:18.310 --> 00:53:20.350 align:middle line:84%
is don't allow two stressed
syllables in a row.

00:53:20.350 --> 00:53:23.140 align:middle line:84%
And it's at the bottom
because, well, Passamaquoddy

00:53:23.140 --> 00:53:27.813 align:middle line:84%
has two stressed syllables
in a row all the time.

00:53:27.813 --> 00:53:29.980 align:middle line:84%
Impressionistically what's
going on in Passamaquoddy

00:53:29.980 --> 00:53:31.390 align:middle line:84%
is you stress the
first syllable,

00:53:31.390 --> 00:53:33.700 align:middle line:84%
and you stress every other
syllable counting backwards

00:53:33.700 --> 00:53:35.900 align:middle line:90%
from the end of the word.

00:53:35.900 --> 00:53:41.690 align:middle line:84%
So "dirt" is "tupqan," and then
"in the dirt" is "tupqanok."

00:53:41.690 --> 00:53:43.810 align:middle line:84%
So you get stress on
the first two syllables,

00:53:43.810 --> 00:53:48.340 align:middle line:84%
more stress actually on the
second one than on the first.

00:53:48.340 --> 00:53:50.547 align:middle line:84%
I wish that this were not
the Passamaquoddy stress

00:53:50.547 --> 00:53:52.630 align:middle line:84%
system, because I'm trying
to learn Passamaquoddy.

00:53:52.630 --> 00:53:54.880 align:middle line:84%
And it means that
if you're trying

00:53:54.880 --> 00:53:57.970 align:middle line:84%
to say something like
"Let's walk around on top,"

00:53:57.970 --> 00:53:59.890 align:middle line:84%
you have to ask
yourself, OK, is there

00:53:59.890 --> 00:54:02.820 align:middle line:84%
an odd number of syllables in
that word or an even number,

00:54:02.820 --> 00:54:04.570 align:middle line:84%
because that's how you
know whether to say

00:54:04.570 --> 00:54:08.560 align:middle line:84%
"tehsahqapasultine,"
with stress,

00:54:08.560 --> 00:54:11.800 align:middle line:84%
main stress on the next-to-last
syllable, with two stressed

00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:15.860 align:middle line:84%
syllables at the
beginning, or not.

00:54:15.860 --> 00:54:18.810 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so it's a pain.

00:54:18.810 --> 00:54:27.021 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, or here's Tauya, where,
again, we could decompose this.

00:54:27.021 --> 00:54:29.910 align:middle line:84%
This is not unlike
Passamaquoddy,

00:54:29.910 --> 00:54:32.610 align:middle line:84%
except you're stressing
the first syllable.

00:54:32.610 --> 00:54:36.060 align:middle line:84%
And you are stressing
the last syllable.

00:54:36.060 --> 00:54:38.520 align:middle line:84%
And then you are stressing
every other syllable counting

00:54:38.520 --> 00:54:40.350 align:middle line:90%
backwards from the end.

00:54:40.350 --> 00:54:43.840 align:middle line:84%
So you end up with bi-syllables
having two stresses,

00:54:43.840 --> 00:54:45.840 align:middle line:84%
unlike in Passamaquoddy,
where there's only one.

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:48.695 align:middle line:90%


00:54:48.695 --> 00:54:50.070 align:middle line:84%
That's one kind
of stress system,

00:54:50.070 --> 00:54:52.440 align:middle line:84%
these kinds of stress systems
where you have repeated

00:54:52.440 --> 00:54:53.850 align:middle line:90%
stresses.

00:54:53.850 --> 00:54:56.085 align:middle line:84%
There's another kind
of stress system

00:54:56.085 --> 00:54:58.710 align:middle line:84%
that's going to have to include
constraints that make reference

00:54:58.710 --> 00:55:00.100 align:middle line:90%
to particular morphemes.

00:55:00.100 --> 00:55:02.610 align:middle line:84%
So I want to give you
an example of that.

00:55:02.610 --> 00:55:05.280 align:middle line:84%
The classic example that
I know of is from Russian.

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:09.060 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to show
you some Russian data.

00:55:09.060 --> 00:55:10.560 align:middle line:84%
English has examples
like this, too.

00:55:10.560 --> 00:55:12.143 align:middle line:84%
We've talked about
it before, the fact

00:55:12.143 --> 00:55:14.700 align:middle line:84%
that "electric" has stress
on its second syllable,

00:55:14.700 --> 00:55:16.970 align:middle line:84%
but that when you
add the suffix "ity,"

00:55:16.970 --> 00:55:18.750 align:middle line:84%
you do various
interesting things.

00:55:18.750 --> 00:55:21.270 align:middle line:84%
You change the "k" sound
at the end of electric

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:23.140 align:middle line:84%
to an "s," so you
get "electricity."

00:55:23.140 --> 00:55:27.870 align:middle line:84%
But also you shift the stress,
so it's not "electricity,"

00:55:27.870 --> 00:55:32.400 align:middle line:84%
it's "electricity" with stress
on what was an "ik" and is now

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:34.380 align:middle line:90%
an "is."

00:55:34.380 --> 00:55:38.310 align:middle line:84%
For Russian, the basic stress
rule goes something like this.

00:55:38.310 --> 00:55:41.100 align:middle line:84%
Russian morphemes
get to indicate

00:55:41.100 --> 00:55:43.770 align:middle line:84%
whether they want
stress or not, and where

00:55:43.770 --> 00:55:45.330 align:middle line:90%
they want the stress to be.

00:55:45.330 --> 00:55:48.803 align:middle line:84%
And the basic rule goes, stress
the first lexically-accented

00:55:48.803 --> 00:55:50.970 align:middle line:84%
syllable, that is, the first
syllable that is marked

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:52.680 align:middle line:90%
as saying, hey, I need stress.

00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:55.200 align:middle line:84%
If there aren't any
lexically-accented syllables,

00:55:55.200 --> 00:55:57.660 align:middle line:84%
then you stress the first
syllable of the word.

00:55:57.660 --> 00:56:00.210 align:middle line:84%
Russian stress is
quite intimidating

00:56:00.210 --> 00:56:01.920 align:middle line:84%
if you're trying
to learn Russian.

00:56:01.920 --> 00:56:04.145 align:middle line:84%
So if you're interested in
trying to learn Russian,

00:56:04.145 --> 00:56:06.270 align:middle line:84%
please try to remember this
rule that will save you

00:56:06.270 --> 00:56:07.990 align:middle line:90%
a lot of heartache.

00:56:07.990 --> 00:56:10.120 align:middle line:90%
So here are some examples.

00:56:10.120 --> 00:56:14.257 align:middle line:84%
The word for town, "gorod,"
is an unaccented word.

00:56:14.257 --> 00:56:16.590 align:middle line:84%
And that means that it has
stress on the first syllable,

00:56:16.590 --> 00:56:19.320 align:middle line:84%
because the rule is stress the
first syllable if there aren't

00:56:19.320 --> 00:56:21.150 align:middle line:84%
any lexically-accented
syllables.

00:56:21.150 --> 00:56:23.280 align:middle line:84%
And the dative
singular suffix, "-u,"

00:56:23.280 --> 00:56:25.650 align:middle line:84%
that's showing up for
this class of nouns,

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:28.140 align:middle line:84%
is also lexically unaccented,
so it doesn't do anything

00:56:28.140 --> 00:56:28.870 align:middle line:90%
to the stress.

00:56:28.870 --> 00:56:31.410 align:middle line:84%
So "gorod" is the word
for town, and "gorodu"

00:56:31.410 --> 00:56:32.430 align:middle line:90%
is the dative singular.

00:56:32.430 --> 00:56:34.890 align:middle line:84%
And again, don't worry about
what dative singular means.

00:56:34.890 --> 00:56:35.922 align:middle line:90%
It's a thing.

00:56:35.922 --> 00:56:37.380 align:middle line:84%
It's a marker you
can put on nouns.

00:56:37.380 --> 00:56:40.540 align:middle line:90%


00:56:40.540 --> 00:56:44.440 align:middle line:84%
But the dative plural suffix,
putting it in a different form,

00:56:44.440 --> 00:56:47.350 align:middle line:90%
is a lexically accented stress.

00:56:47.350 --> 00:56:48.820 align:middle line:90%
It does require stress.

00:56:48.820 --> 00:56:51.460 align:middle line:84%
And so stress shifts to
that particular suffix.

00:56:51.460 --> 00:56:53.410 align:middle line:84%
So you get initial
stress because there's

00:56:53.410 --> 00:56:55.810 align:middle line:84%
no lexical accent
in gorod and gorodu,

00:56:55.810 --> 00:56:57.550 align:middle line:84%
but if you add the
data plural suffix,

00:56:57.550 --> 00:57:00.880 align:middle line:84%
you get "gorodam," with the
stress shifting to the end,

00:57:00.880 --> 00:57:03.850 align:middle line:84%
because this is a suffix
that demands stress.

00:57:03.850 --> 00:57:05.470 align:middle line:84%
There are also nouns
that want stress

00:57:05.470 --> 00:57:06.850 align:middle line:90%
on a particular syllable.

00:57:06.850 --> 00:57:10.780 align:middle line:84%
So the word for nut,
which is "orex," wants--

00:57:10.780 --> 00:57:12.970 align:middle line:84%
has lexical accent on
its second syllable.

00:57:12.970 --> 00:57:16.200 align:middle line:84%
And that means that, no matter
what you add to that noun,

00:57:16.200 --> 00:57:17.980 align:middle line:84%
the stress is not
going to shift.

00:57:17.980 --> 00:57:20.440 align:middle line:90%
So the word for nut is "orex."

00:57:20.440 --> 00:57:23.200 align:middle line:84%
And if it's dative singular,
if it's dative plural,

00:57:23.200 --> 00:57:25.300 align:middle line:84%
you still get stress on
that second syllable,

00:57:25.300 --> 00:57:28.450 align:middle line:84%
because the basic rule is stress
the first lexically-accented

00:57:28.450 --> 00:57:29.440 align:middle line:90%
syllable.

00:57:29.440 --> 00:57:33.037 align:middle line:84%
So in the last example,
the dative plural of nut,

00:57:33.037 --> 00:57:34.870 align:middle line:84%
the stress is still in
that second syllable.

00:57:34.870 --> 00:57:37.420 align:middle line:84%
It's "orexam," even
though, as we've seen, "am"

00:57:37.420 --> 00:57:38.860 align:middle line:90%
attracts stress to itself.

00:57:38.860 --> 00:57:40.360 align:middle line:84%
It only attracts
stress to itself

00:57:40.360 --> 00:57:43.528 align:middle line:84%
from nouns that don't themselves
have lexical accent, yeah.

00:57:43.528 --> 00:57:45.070 align:middle line:84%
There, now you know
more about that--

00:57:45.070 --> 00:57:47.230 align:middle line:84%
I tried years ago to
try to learn Russian.

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:49.450 align:middle line:84%
And this was one of the
things that made me give up.

00:57:49.450 --> 00:57:51.850 align:middle line:84%
It was like the stress just
doesn't make sense to me.

00:57:51.850 --> 00:57:53.990 align:middle line:90%
I don't understand it.

00:57:53.990 --> 00:57:56.080 align:middle line:84%
But now you know,
so and I do too.

00:57:56.080 --> 00:57:58.780 align:middle line:84%
I should go try
to learn it again.

00:57:58.780 --> 00:58:00.355 align:middle line:84%
So one way to
think about this is

00:58:00.355 --> 00:58:04.330 align:middle line:84%
you say a Russian morpheme
can have a lexical accent.

00:58:04.330 --> 00:58:08.510 align:middle line:84%
As part of its specification, it
can have a little note saying,

00:58:08.510 --> 00:58:10.670 align:middle line:90%
hey, I want stress to be here.

00:58:10.670 --> 00:58:12.910 align:middle line:84%
And then we need constraints
that say things like,

00:58:12.910 --> 00:58:15.700 align:middle line:84%
there's only one
stress in a word.

00:58:15.700 --> 00:58:17.800 align:middle line:84%
You want to stress
lexical accents,

00:58:17.800 --> 00:58:21.350 align:middle line:84%
and you should put your stress
as far to the left as possible.

00:58:21.350 --> 00:58:25.840 align:middle line:84%
So in a word like town, there
aren't any lexical accents.

00:58:25.840 --> 00:58:28.633 align:middle line:84%
And so the only relevant
constraints, I guess,

00:58:28.633 --> 00:58:29.800 align:middle line:90%
are the first and the third.

00:58:29.800 --> 00:58:31.030 align:middle line:84%
There should be one
stress, and it should

00:58:31.030 --> 00:58:32.200 align:middle line:90%
be as far left as possible.

00:58:32.200 --> 00:58:35.410 align:middle line:84%
So it's at the
beginning, "gorod."

00:58:35.410 --> 00:58:39.220 align:middle line:84%
For "gorodam," the
dative plural of town,

00:58:39.220 --> 00:58:40.780 align:middle line:90%
there can only be one stress.

00:58:40.780 --> 00:58:42.530 align:middle line:84%
You want it to be as
far left as possible.

00:58:42.530 --> 00:58:45.340 align:middle line:84%
But there's a lexical accent
on dative plural suffix.

00:58:45.340 --> 00:58:47.590 align:middle line:90%
And so you put it on "am."

00:58:47.590 --> 00:58:50.020 align:middle line:84%
that's as far left
as you can go, yeah.

00:58:50.020 --> 00:58:52.870 align:middle line:84%
So the last constraint
doesn't get to do very much.

00:58:52.870 --> 00:58:56.320 align:middle line:84%
And then for nut,
there's lexical accent

00:58:56.320 --> 00:58:58.600 align:middle line:84%
on the "ex," on the second
syllable of the noun.

00:58:58.600 --> 00:59:01.275 align:middle line:84%
And because you're putting
stress as far left as possible,

00:59:01.275 --> 00:59:02.650 align:middle line:84%
that's where the
stress is always

00:59:02.650 --> 00:59:04.540 align:middle line:84%
going to go, no matter
what suffixes you add.

00:59:04.540 --> 00:59:06.250 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's the basic picture.

00:59:06.250 --> 00:59:09.080 align:middle line:90%


00:59:09.080 --> 00:59:13.670 align:middle line:84%
OK, we will do a little bit of
Kashmiri and then we'll stop,

00:59:13.670 --> 00:59:15.410 align:middle line:90%
I think, for today.

00:59:15.410 --> 00:59:18.050 align:middle line:84%
This actually came up when we
were talking about cucumbers.

00:59:18.050 --> 00:59:20.210 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
that have stress systems

00:59:20.210 --> 00:59:22.470 align:middle line:84%
that care about what's
called syllable weight.

00:59:22.470 --> 00:59:25.910 align:middle line:84%
That is, they care about
whether vowels are long or short

00:59:25.910 --> 00:59:27.950 align:middle line:84%
and also about how
many consonants there

00:59:27.950 --> 00:59:30.650 align:middle line:90%
are after vowels.

00:59:30.650 --> 00:59:33.270 align:middle line:84%
And Kashmiri is a
classic example of this.

00:59:33.270 --> 00:59:35.720 align:middle line:84%
And I'll show you a
little bit of this

00:59:35.720 --> 00:59:38.030 align:middle line:84%
and we'll see whether we
can find a good stopping

00:59:38.030 --> 00:59:41.420 align:middle line:90%
place somewhere in the middle.

00:59:41.420 --> 00:59:42.680 align:middle line:90%
Here are some Kashmiri words.

00:59:42.680 --> 00:59:45.097 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of Kashmiri
words that have initial stress.

00:59:45.097 --> 00:59:48.562 align:middle line:84%
Anybody here speak Kashmiri,
language of Kashmir?

00:59:48.562 --> 00:59:51.450 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:59:51.450 --> 00:59:53.312 align:middle line:84%
So lots of words
have initial stress.

00:59:53.312 --> 00:59:55.020 align:middle line:84%
Basically, initial
stress is what you get

00:59:55.020 --> 00:59:57.250 align:middle line:90%
if nothing else is happening.

00:59:57.250 --> 00:59:58.920 align:middle line:84%
But if there's a long
vowel in the word,

00:59:58.920 --> 01:00:00.920 align:middle line:84%
even if it's not the first
syllable of the word,

01:00:00.920 --> 01:00:03.330 align:middle line:84%
then the long vowel
will get stress.

01:00:03.330 --> 01:00:07.653 align:middle line:84%
So that verb, to finish, has a
long vowel as its third vowel,

01:00:07.653 --> 01:00:09.570 align:middle line:84%
and that's where the
stress goes in that word.

01:00:09.570 --> 01:00:12.930 align:middle line:90%


01:00:12.930 --> 01:00:15.750 align:middle line:84%
A long vowel will attract
stress except when it's final.

01:00:15.750 --> 01:00:20.230 align:middle line:84%
So if it's the last vowel in the
word, it doesn't get stressed.

01:00:20.230 --> 01:00:22.382 align:middle line:84%
So the word for book
has two syllables.

01:00:22.382 --> 01:00:24.090 align:middle line:84%
And the second syllable
has a long vowel.

01:00:24.090 --> 01:00:27.075 align:middle line:84%
But the first syllable gets
the stress, because Kashmiri

01:00:27.075 --> 01:00:28.950 align:middle line:84%
like several other
languages we've looked at,

01:00:28.950 --> 01:00:32.050 align:middle line:84%
doesn't like stressing
final syllables.

01:00:32.050 --> 01:00:36.820 align:middle line:84%
If there are two long vowels,
then you stress the first one.

01:00:36.820 --> 01:00:38.755 align:middle line:84%
And another kind of thing
that attracts stress

01:00:38.755 --> 01:00:40.630 align:middle line:84%
is a vowel that's followed
by two consonants.

01:00:40.630 --> 01:00:44.320 align:middle line:84%
So here's the word for "Friday,"
where the second vowel is

01:00:44.320 --> 01:00:46.038 align:middle line:90%
followed by an "r" and a "v."

01:00:46.038 --> 01:00:47.830 align:middle line:84%
It's the first example,
I guess, that we've

01:00:47.830 --> 01:00:50.337 align:middle line:84%
had of a vowel that's
followed by two consonants.

01:00:50.337 --> 01:00:52.420 align:middle line:84%
And a vowel that's followed
by two consonants also

01:00:52.420 --> 01:00:54.160 align:middle line:90%
preferentially gets stressed.

01:00:54.160 --> 01:00:57.080 align:middle line:90%
It acts like a long vowel.

01:00:57.080 --> 01:00:59.080 align:middle line:84%
If you have both a long
vowel and a vowel that's

01:00:59.080 --> 01:01:01.900 align:middle line:84%
followed by two consonants,
then you prefer the long vowel.

01:01:01.900 --> 01:01:05.830 align:middle line:84%
So you're getting
that in here in door.

01:01:05.830 --> 01:01:08.185 align:middle line:84%
In Friday, you're not getting
stress on the long vowel.

01:01:08.185 --> 01:01:10.810 align:middle line:84%
That's because the long vowel is
the last syllable of the word,

01:01:10.810 --> 01:01:14.600 align:middle line:84%
and the more important thing is
don't stress the last syllable.

01:01:14.600 --> 01:01:17.380 align:middle line:84%
So we can fruitfully
think of Kashmiri stress

01:01:17.380 --> 01:01:18.820 align:middle line:84%
as a bunch of
ranked constraints,

01:01:18.820 --> 01:01:21.400 align:middle line:84%
again, which say
things like don't

01:01:21.400 --> 01:01:22.790 align:middle line:90%
have more than one stress.

01:01:22.790 --> 01:01:24.640 align:middle line:90%
Don't stress the final syllable.

01:01:24.640 --> 01:01:26.260 align:middle line:90%
Stress your long vowels.

01:01:26.260 --> 01:01:28.870 align:middle line:84%
Stress your vowels that have
multiple consonants after them,

01:01:28.870 --> 01:01:30.820 align:middle line:84%
and then put stress
as far to the left

01:01:30.820 --> 01:01:32.770 align:middle line:90%
in the word as possible.

01:01:32.770 --> 01:01:35.980 align:middle line:84%
So stress long vowels,
we got that in that word

01:01:35.980 --> 01:01:39.490 align:middle line:84%
that had a long vowel
as its third vowel.

01:01:39.490 --> 01:01:42.310 align:middle line:84%
But we also saw that
outranking stress

01:01:42.310 --> 01:01:44.007 align:middle line:84%
long vowels is a
constraint that says

01:01:44.007 --> 01:01:45.340 align:middle line:90%
don't stress the final syllable.

01:01:45.340 --> 01:01:47.493 align:middle line:84%
So in this word
for "book," there's

01:01:47.493 --> 01:01:48.910 align:middle line:84%
a long vowel in
the final syllable

01:01:48.910 --> 01:01:50.743 align:middle line:84%
but you don't stress
it, because it's final.

01:01:50.743 --> 01:01:53.230 align:middle line:84%
And avoiding final stress is
more important than stressing

01:01:53.230 --> 01:01:55.540 align:middle line:90%
long vowels.

01:01:55.540 --> 01:01:58.090 align:middle line:84%
There's a rule that says don't
have more than one stress.

01:01:58.090 --> 01:02:00.402 align:middle line:84%
So you like stressing
long vowels,

01:02:00.402 --> 01:02:02.860 align:middle line:84%
but if you have a word that
has multiple long vowels in it,

01:02:02.860 --> 01:02:03.940 align:middle line:90%
you only get one stress.

01:02:03.940 --> 01:02:06.910 align:middle line:90%
You don't get two, yeah?

01:02:06.910 --> 01:02:09.660 align:middle line:84%
And the general rule is, put
stress as far to the left

01:02:09.660 --> 01:02:10.660 align:middle line:90%
in the word as possible.

01:02:10.660 --> 01:02:12.910 align:middle line:84%
So when you have two long
vowels you stress the first one.

01:02:12.910 --> 01:02:13.990 align:middle line:90%
You can't stress them both.

01:02:13.990 --> 01:02:15.370 align:middle line:84%
You also don't stress
the second one.

01:02:15.370 --> 01:02:17.370 align:middle line:84%
You stress the first one
because you want stress

01:02:17.370 --> 01:02:19.120 align:middle line:84%
to be as far to the
left as possible.

01:02:19.120 --> 01:02:21.820 align:middle line:84%
That's also why, when there are
no long vowels, and no vowels

01:02:21.820 --> 01:02:25.270 align:middle line:84%
with multiple consonants after
them, you get initial stress.

01:02:25.270 --> 01:02:27.820 align:middle line:84%
And then finally, stressed
vowels with multiple consonants

01:02:27.820 --> 01:02:30.070 align:middle line:84%
after them, that's got
to be in there somewhere.

01:02:30.070 --> 01:02:32.500 align:middle line:84%
And it's got to be below
stressed long vowels,

01:02:32.500 --> 01:02:36.190 align:middle line:84%
because when you have both a
vowel with multiple consonants

01:02:36.190 --> 01:02:39.213 align:middle line:84%
after it and a long vowel,
you stress the long vowel

01:02:39.213 --> 01:02:41.380 align:middle line:84%
and not the vowel with
multiple consonants after it.

01:02:41.380 --> 01:02:43.900 align:middle line:84%
So here's another
place where it's

01:02:43.900 --> 01:02:49.750 align:middle line:84%
possible to describe Kashmiri
stress in several paragraphs

01:02:49.750 --> 01:02:52.930 align:middle line:84%
of prose, in which
you say things

01:02:52.930 --> 01:02:56.200 align:middle line:84%
like, well, Kashmiri stress,
it's on the first syllable

01:02:56.200 --> 01:02:59.080 align:middle line:84%
unless there are long vowels or
vowels with multiple consonants

01:02:59.080 --> 01:03:01.300 align:middle line:84%
after them, except
when those are final

01:03:01.300 --> 01:03:04.525 align:middle line:84%
or on a Tuesday in a
month beginning with "m."

01:03:04.525 --> 01:03:08.080 align:middle line:84%
It would be quite
long and complicated.

01:03:08.080 --> 01:03:10.240 align:middle line:84%
If we describe Kashmiri
stress this way in terms

01:03:10.240 --> 01:03:12.430 align:middle line:84%
of ranked constraints,
the constraints themselves

01:03:12.430 --> 01:03:13.640 align:middle line:90%
can all be pretty simple.

01:03:13.640 --> 01:03:15.640 align:middle line:84%
They can be things like
stress your long vowels.

01:03:15.640 --> 01:03:17.740 align:middle line:84%
That's a pretty
natural thing to do.

01:03:17.740 --> 01:03:19.450 align:middle line:90%
Or avoid final stress.

01:03:19.450 --> 01:03:22.670 align:middle line:84%
Or put your stress as far to the
left in the word as possible.

01:03:22.670 --> 01:03:24.910 align:middle line:84%
And all of the action
is figuring out

01:03:24.910 --> 01:03:26.415 align:middle line:84%
what order are these
constraints in,

01:03:26.415 --> 01:03:27.790 align:middle line:84%
which are the most
important ones

01:03:27.790 --> 01:03:29.020 align:middle line:90%
and which are less important.

01:03:29.020 --> 01:03:30.400 align:middle line:84%
And by ranking
them that way, you

01:03:30.400 --> 01:03:32.943 align:middle line:84%
end up with this quite
complicated array

01:03:32.943 --> 01:03:35.110 align:middle line:84%
of data, which we've now
gone through quite quickly.

01:03:35.110 --> 01:03:37.090 align:middle line:90%
But you'll have the slides.

01:03:37.090 --> 01:03:40.510 align:middle line:84%
So you'll have a chance to go
through this in your own time.

01:03:40.510 --> 01:03:42.130 align:middle line:84%
Are the questions
about Kashmiri?

01:03:42.130 --> 01:03:43.780 align:middle line:84%
Since we have a few
more seconds here,

01:03:43.780 --> 01:03:45.520 align:middle line:84%
we can talk about
Kashmiri in more depth

01:03:45.520 --> 01:03:48.332 align:middle line:84%
if people would like,
or anything else.

01:03:48.332 --> 01:03:50.290 align:middle line:84%
As I said at the beginning,
I could hear people

01:03:50.290 --> 01:03:51.190 align:middle line:90%
talking about the problem set.

01:03:51.190 --> 01:03:52.570 align:middle line:84%
If people have questions
about the problem set,

01:03:52.570 --> 01:03:53.528 align:middle line:90%
we can talk about them.

01:03:53.528 --> 01:03:54.195 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:03:54.195 --> 01:03:57.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The chapter
felt like-- so is, like,

01:03:57.550 --> 01:03:58.270 align:middle line:90%
for like English.

01:03:58.270 --> 01:03:59.170 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:03:59.170 --> 01:04:00.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there like
a system like this?

01:04:00.670 --> 01:04:01.090 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh.

01:04:01.090 --> 01:04:01.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Or is it
just kind of like--

01:04:01.780 --> 01:04:02.500 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So, yeah, here I

01:04:02.500 --> 01:04:03.850 align:middle line:90%
am talking about stress systems.

01:04:03.850 --> 01:04:05.538 align:middle line:84%
And I've talked to
you about Kashmiri

01:04:05.538 --> 01:04:07.330 align:middle line:84%
and I've talked to you
about Passamaquoddy.

01:04:07.330 --> 01:04:09.497 align:middle line:84%
I've talked to you about
various languages, Russian,

01:04:09.497 --> 01:04:13.270 align:middle line:84%
and I've strenuously avoided
talking to you about English.

01:04:13.270 --> 01:04:16.820 align:middle line:84%
English stress is quite
complicated and messy.

01:04:16.820 --> 01:04:18.980 align:middle line:90%
There are books about it.

01:04:18.980 --> 01:04:21.310 align:middle line:84%
It's partly, like so
much about English,

01:04:21.310 --> 01:04:26.470 align:middle line:84%
the product of several different
languages with different stress

01:04:26.470 --> 01:04:29.600 align:middle line:84%
systems interacting
with each other.

01:04:29.600 --> 01:04:33.280 align:middle line:84%
So there was a
Germanic stress system

01:04:33.280 --> 01:04:36.760 align:middle line:84%
that English inherited from
its Germanic substrate.

01:04:36.760 --> 01:04:40.270 align:middle line:84%
And then the French
invaded in 1066

01:04:40.270 --> 01:04:42.110 align:middle line:84%
bringing their
own stress system.

01:04:42.110 --> 01:04:45.130 align:middle line:84%
And one of the
results of that is

01:04:45.130 --> 01:04:47.200 align:middle line:84%
that English stress
is the kind of thing

01:04:47.200 --> 01:04:48.462 align:middle line:90%
you can write books about.

01:04:48.462 --> 01:04:50.170 align:middle line:84%
So there's a reason
I haven't tried to do

01:04:50.170 --> 01:04:51.520 align:middle line:90%
English stress on the slide.

01:04:51.520 --> 01:04:54.790 align:middle line:84%
You think Kashmiri
is complicated, yeah.

01:04:54.790 --> 01:04:59.050 align:middle line:90%
It's, yeah, it's complicated.

01:04:59.050 --> 01:05:00.640 align:middle line:84%
It's complicated in
interesting ways.

01:05:00.640 --> 01:05:02.420 align:middle line:84%
We have various
places, for example,

01:05:02.420 --> 01:05:05.110 align:middle line:84%
where the difference
between a verb and a noun

01:05:05.110 --> 01:05:06.700 align:middle line:90%
has to do with stress.

01:05:06.700 --> 01:05:12.220 align:middle line:84%
So we have verbs like "perMIT"
and nouns like "PERmit," right?

01:05:12.220 --> 01:05:15.100 align:middle line:90%
Get a permit to do something.

01:05:15.100 --> 01:05:17.850 align:middle line:84%
And there are a number
of pairs like that,

01:05:17.850 --> 01:05:19.600 align:middle line:84%
which is one of the
kinds of things that's

01:05:19.600 --> 01:05:20.725 align:middle line:90%
interesting to think about.

01:05:20.725 --> 01:05:22.540 align:middle line:84%
That's not something
that we inherited.

01:05:22.540 --> 01:05:27.010 align:middle line:84%
And so when people are
trying to figure out how--

01:05:27.010 --> 01:05:29.680 align:middle line:84%
we talked earlier
about the possibility

01:05:29.680 --> 01:05:32.170 align:middle line:84%
that nouns and verbs could
have different phonologies,

01:05:32.170 --> 01:05:34.300 align:middle line:84%
and there are times
when something like that

01:05:34.300 --> 01:05:37.330 align:middle line:84%
seems to be happening, like
in that kind of example.

01:05:37.330 --> 01:05:39.988 align:middle line:84%
So when you write your book
on English stress, that's

01:05:39.988 --> 01:05:41.530 align:middle line:84%
one of the chapters
you have to write

01:05:41.530 --> 01:05:43.570 align:middle line:84%
is the difference between
permit and permit.

01:05:43.570 --> 01:05:44.530 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:05:44.530 --> 01:05:45.298 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:05:45.298 --> 01:05:47.173 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You said, is
there like some method

01:05:47.173 --> 01:05:49.360 align:middle line:84%
we can use to define
the like all the rules

01:05:49.360 --> 01:05:50.110 align:middle line:90%
that were in this?

01:05:50.110 --> 01:05:52.770 align:middle line:84%
Like adjusting rules to
see what the basis is.

01:05:52.770 --> 01:05:54.960 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
what you want to do

01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:57.270 align:middle line:84%
is look systematically
at each vowel.

01:05:57.270 --> 01:05:59.520 align:middle line:84%
Here's what I would
do in your shoes.

01:05:59.520 --> 01:06:01.290 align:middle line:84%
Look systematically
at each vowel

01:06:01.290 --> 01:06:05.010 align:middle line:84%
and make yourself notes for each
vowel about the environments

01:06:05.010 --> 01:06:06.480 align:middle line:90%
that they show up in.

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:11.040 align:middle line:84%
So, say you start
with the vowel "a."

01:06:11.040 --> 01:06:14.952 align:middle line:84%
Make yourself notes about all of
the things that are around "a."

01:06:14.952 --> 01:06:16.410 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you could
start with the hope

01:06:16.410 --> 01:06:18.977 align:middle line:84%
that the relevant things are
the consonants on either side

01:06:18.977 --> 01:06:21.060 align:middle line:84%
of it, or whether it's at
the beginning or the end

01:06:21.060 --> 01:06:23.435 align:middle line:84%
or the first syllable or the
second syllable or whatever.

01:06:23.435 --> 01:06:26.730 align:middle line:84%
Make yourself notes
for each word that

01:06:26.730 --> 01:06:29.228 align:middle line:84%
has an "i" in it, where
the "i" is showing up.

01:06:29.228 --> 01:06:31.770 align:middle line:84%
And then look at your notes and
hope that something jumps out

01:06:31.770 --> 01:06:32.700 align:middle line:90%
at you.

01:06:32.700 --> 01:06:34.782 align:middle line:84%
That's the only thing I
can think of to suggest.

01:06:34.782 --> 01:06:36.490 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, that's probably
what you're doing.

01:06:36.490 --> 01:06:39.256 align:middle line:84%
But if that's what you're
doing, you're doing well.

01:06:39.256 --> 01:06:42.810 align:middle line:90%
Are there other questions?

01:06:42.810 --> 01:06:45.630 align:middle line:84%
OK, all right, so I'll
see you on Thursday

01:06:45.630 --> 01:06:48.230 align:middle line:84%
when we will start
talking about syntax.

01:06:48.230 --> 01:06:57.000 align:middle line:90%